-- postpartum week 23: Biotin, ordinary food and smarter supplement choices
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   23,
   NULL,
   'Phase 3 of 6 — Rebuilding',
   'Biotin, ordinary food and smarter supplement choices',
   '"Food usually provides what you need — and supplement labels still matter."',
   'Biotin (vitamin B7) is a cofactor for enzymes your body uses to turn fat, carbohydrate, and protein into usable energy, and it also plays a role in how your cells read and use genetic information. It''s also one of the most heavily marketed vitamins for hair, skin, and nail health — a claim that reaches well beyond what the actual evidence supports for most healthy adults. Your daily reference: ICMR-NIN''s 2020 report gives biotin only an Adequate Intake (AI), not an RDA — like a few other nutrients in this series, the evidence wasn''t sufficient to set a full requirement figure. The AI is 40 mcg a day for a general adult woman, staying the same in pregnancy, and rising to 45 mcg a day while breastfeeding, a 5 mcg a day increase for losses through breast milk. NIH''s US reference is also AI-only, at a different baseline: 30 mcg a day for a non-pregnant woman, staying the same in pregnancy, and rising to 35 mcg a day while breastfeeding. These are two different reference systems, not a single target — but both agree on the same shape: no increase needed for pregnancy, and a small, easily covered increase for breastfeeding. If you are breastfeeding or expressing milk: The additional 5 mcg reference during breastfeeding is modest and can be supported through an ordinary varied pattern. Cooked egg, fish, or meat can contribute if chosen, while seeds, nuts, legumes, and vegetables such as sweet potato also form part of a vegetarian pathway. One imperfect day doesn''t change the overall pattern. If you are not breastfeeding: If you''re not breastfeeding, biotin remains useful for everyday energy metabolism, and your daily reference returns to the general adult figure. Where biotin comes from: One cooked egg provides roughly 10 mcg of biotin — about one-quarter of the ICMR-NIN adult AI of 40 mcg, or about one-third of the NIH adult AI of 30 mcg. Fish, meat, sunflower seeds, sweet potato, and almonds also contribute meaningfully; most fruits and refined breads contribute very little. Raw egg white contains avidin, a protein that can bind biotin and reduce its absorption. Cooking denatures avidin, and fully cooked eggs are also the safer choice during pregnancy and postpartum. A gentle note for this week: No Tolerable Upper Limit has been established because direct toxicity has not been demonstrated. However, high-dose biotin supplements are not risk-free: they can distort important laboratory results and lead to incorrect clinical interpretation. Do not add a high-dose product for this week''s theme unless it has been specifically advised. Biotin supplements can interfere with some laboratory tests, including certain thyroid and heart-related tests. The risk depends on the dose, product, test method, and time since the last dose. Tell your clinician and the laboratory the exact product, dose, and time you last took it, and follow their instructions about whether and when it should be paused — don''t stop a prescribed product on your own. If you have urgent symptoms such as chest pain, breathlessness, fainting, or severe weakness, seek urgent care and tell the team about the biotin — don''t delay testing. This week''s rhythm: Because biotin is spread across a genuinely wide range of protein foods, seeds, and a few vegetables, a normal, varied pattern of meals covers it comfortably — cooked egg is simply an easy, reliable anchor if you eat it. There''s no need to plan meals specifically around this nutrient. Each day below offers two options for every meal — pick whichever suits you. Cooked egg, when it appears, is a strong single-food source, but it isn''t the only way to reach this week''s reference. If you are managing a health condition - Thyroid conditions: Ordinary biotin-containing foods have no established link to thyroid function. But if you''re taking a biotin-containing supplement (including a B-complex or a hair, skin, or nail product) and have a thyroid blood test coming up, tell your lab and doctor the exact product, dose, and time you last took it — biotin supplements are a documented, real cause of inaccurate thyroid test results — and follow their instructions about whether and when it should be paused. - High blood pressure, previous pre-eclampsia, diabetes, recent gestational diabetes, or PCOS: These do not require a special restriction on ordinary biotin-containing foods. Continue the eating pattern, medication, and monitoring already advised for your condition. - Kidney disease: Ordinary food sources aren''t a specific concern in kidney disease, but dialysis and individual renal diets can change vitamin needs. Follow your renal team''s advice before adding any supplement.',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Biotin (vitamin B7) is a cofactor for enzymes your body uses to turn fat, carbohydrate, and protein into usable energy, and it also plays a role in how your cells read and use genetic information. It''s also one of the most heavily marketed vitamins for hair, skin, and nail health — a claim that reaches well beyond what the actual evidence supports for most healthy adults.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla with a side of tomato.", "breakfast_b": "Boiled egg (cooked through) with whole-wheat toast.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a rajma curry and a cabbage sabzi.", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a beans sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted almonds with a seasonal fruit.", "nourishment_break_b": "Sunflower seeds with a banana.", "dinner_a": "Sweet potato and vegetable sabzi with a whole-wheat roti.", "dinner_b": "A curry made with a locally available fish from the lower-mercury category, with rice and a cucumber salad.", "still_hungry": "Whatever''s easiest to reach for right now."}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "A small step up while breastfeeding", "notes": "ICMR-NIN''s reference rises by 5 mcg a day while you''re breastfeeding — a small increase, comfortably covered through an ordinary varied pattern of meals, whether or not that includes egg or fish. About your prenatal or postnatal supplement: Biotin appears in many prenatal, postnatal, and B-complex products, sometimes at doses well above what food alone provides. Check your labels, and mention any biotin-containing product to your doctor or lab — especially before a blood test, for the reason described earlier.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with a seasonal fruit.", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (two eggs, cooked through) with whole-wheat toast.", "lunch_a": "Bajra roti with a moong dal and a spinach sabzi.", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with rice and a carrot-and-peas sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with sunflower seeds.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted peanuts with a seasonal fruit.", "dinner_a": "Vegetable khichdi.", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a tomato salad.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Where biotin naturally shows up", "notes": "Biotin appears across several food groups, so today is about variety rather than relying on one ingredient. Choose tolerated foods that already fit your household pattern. If you have a diagnosed allergy: If you have a diagnosed egg, dairy, peanut, seed, or seafood allergy, avoid that food completely and choose one of the many other sources in this week''s chart. If a food causes digestive discomfort rather than an allergy, use only forms and portions you already know you tolerate. Don''t trial a problem food to pursue this week''s nutrient.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam.", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with whole-wheat toast.", "lunch_a": "Ragi roti with a chana sabzi and a small bowl of curd.", "lunch_b": "A curry made with a locally available fish from the lower-mercury category, with rice and a tomato-and-cucumber salad.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted almonds with a banana.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of yogurt with a seasonal fruit.", "dinner_a": "Paneer-and-vegetable stir-fry with a whole-wheat roti.", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with rice and a cabbage sabzi.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Why more isn''t the goal", "notes": "Although no upper limit has been established, high-dose biotin supplements can still interfere with important laboratory tests. Hair, skin, and nail supplements: Biotin supplements are heavily marketed for hair, skin, and nail health, and many mothers notice hair thinning or shedding in the months after birth. But the evidence for biotin supplements in generally healthy adults is limited — based mostly on small studies without a comparison group, or on case reports in children with rare medical conditions. Postpartum hair shedding is commonly related to hormonal changes and often improves with time, but biotin is not a proven general treatment. Mention hair loss to your clinician if it is patchy, causes scalp redness or pain, is severe or worsening, continues longer than expected, or occurs with symptoms such as marked fatigue, dizziness, palpitations, or feeling unusually cold — other causes, including thyroid or iron-related problems, may need assessment. For most mothers eating a varied diet, food usually covers this week''s reference. There is no established reason to add a biotin supplement solely for postpartum hair shedding, and unnecessary high-dose use brings the laboratory-test-interference risk described earlier.", "breakfast_a": "Oats porridge with a seasonal fruit.", "breakfast_b": "Chicken sandwich (whole-wheat bread, fully cooked chicken) with a side of tomato.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a masoor dal and a sweet potato sabzi.", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with rice and a green-peas sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "Sunflower seeds with a seasonal fruit.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a banana.", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti, a moong dal, and a spinach sabzi.", "dinner_b": "A curry made with a locally available fish from the lower-mercury category, with rice and a carrot salad.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "A naturally reassuring nutrient", "notes": "Real biotin deficiency from diet alone is rare. Researchers have observed biochemical signs of lower biotin status during pregnancy and breastfeeding, but the health significance remains uncertain. This finding alone is not a reason to self-supplement. One group worth a mention: if you take anticonvulsant (seizure) medication long-term, these medicines are known to lower biotin levels over time, so it''s worth asking your prescribing clinician whether assessment is needed — never stop or change a prescribed seizure medicine on your own.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya with a seasonal fruit.", "breakfast_b": "Egg paratha (whole-wheat) with a side of curd.", "lunch_a": "Jowar roti with a rajma curry and a beans sabzi.", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a mushroom sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted peanuts with a banana.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with almonds.", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti, a masoor dal, and a potato sabzi.", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with rice and a tomato salad.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Away from home", "notes": "Roasted almonds, sunflower seeds, and roasted chana are naturally easy to carry today — no cold storage needed for these. Food safety: Keep the egg and chicken sandwiches refrigerated or in an insulated bag with an ice pack, and make sure they stay cold until you actually eat them — discard anything that''s been sitting out at room temperature for more than about two hours (about one hour in hot weather). Roasted almonds, sunflower seeds, and roasted chana are shelf-stable and don''t need the same cold-chain care. Reheat the dal curry or mutton curry until steaming hot before eating.", "breakfast_a": "A whole-wheat vegetable sandwich with peanut chutney or curd.", "breakfast_b": "A pre-made boiled-egg sandwich (whole-wheat bread), carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten.", "lunch_a": "A roasted-chana-and-vegetable whole-wheat wrap, carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten.", "lunch_b": "A pre-made chicken sandwich (whole-wheat bread, fully cooked chicken), carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten, with a tomato side.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted almonds, kept portable — no cold storage needed.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted chana with a banana.", "dinner_a": "A reheated moong dal-and-vegetable curry with rice.", "dinner_b": "A reheated mutton curry with rice and a vegetable side.", "still_hungry": "Whatever''s already within reach."}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "A quiet week to notice what already felt manageable, and what you''d want to carry into the weeks ahead. Still in Rebuilding. A steady, varied pattern of meals does more for you here than any single perfect meal.", "breakfast_a": "Whichever breakfast felt best this week — worth repeating.", "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": "Whichever lunch from this week you''d want again.", "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": "Whichever snack worked best for you this week.", "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": "Whichever dinner from this week you''d want again.", "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": "A familiar snack, whatever suits you."}]'::jsonb,
   'Did anything you read this week change how you think about supplements versus food?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another nutrient as you continue rebuilding your own strength.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 24: Phosphorus, calcium's quiet partner in your bones
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   24,
   NULL,
   'Phase 3 of 6 — Rebuilding',
   'Phosphorus, calcium''s quiet partner in your bones',
   '"Wherever protein and calcium show up in your day, phosphorus is usually right there too."',
   'Phosphorus is the second-most abundant mineral in your body after calcium. Together, they form hydroxyapatite, the main structural material in bones and teeth. Phosphorus is also part of every cell membrane in your body and plays a role in how your cells store and use energy. Your daily reference: ICMR-NIN recommends an approximately 1:1 relationship between calcium and phosphorus for people beyond infancy, rather than publishing a separate phosphorus RDA for each maternal stage. Applying that approach gives a corresponding phosphorus reference of about 1,000 mg a day for a general adult woman and about 1,200 mg a day during lactation, following calcium''s own figures. NIH uses a different system: a flat 700 mg a day for adults, pregnancy, and lactation, with no increase at any stage. These are two different reference systems with two different shapes, not just two different numbers, presented honestly rather than picking one. If you are breastfeeding or expressing milk: Under ICMR-NIN''s calcium-linked approach, the corresponding phosphorus reference during lactation is about 1,200 mg a day. NIH''s phosphorus RDA remains 700 mg a day during lactation. These are different systems, and ordinary varied meals usually provide phosphorus without deliberate tracking. If you are not breastfeeding: If you''re not breastfeeding, phosphorus remains useful for everyday bone and cell health, and your daily reference returns to the general adult figure. Where phosphorus comes from: Phosphorus occurs naturally in dairy, dal and other legumes, nuts, seeds, whole grains, eggs, fish, and meat. The amount and absorption vary by food, recipe, and portion, so the complete daily pattern matters more than counting one serving. Naturally-occurring food phosphorus is absorbed at roughly 40-70%, with animal sources absorbed somewhat better than plant sources — some of the phosphorus in grains, dal, and legumes is bound as phytate, which is absorbed less efficiently, though this doesn''t make plant sources inadequate as part of a varied diet. A gentle note for this week: NIH''s upper limit for phosphorus is 4,000 mg a day for a general adult woman, a slightly lower 3,500 mg a day specifically during pregnancy, and back to 4,000 mg a day while breastfeeding. ICMR-NIN doesn''t set an upper limit for phosphorus at all — ordinary food isn''t typically a concern in someone with healthy kidneys. Reduced kidney function changes this picture (see the condition note below), and concentrated products like certain laxatives are a separate consideration from ordinary meals. This week''s rhythm: Because phosphorus naturally travels alongside protein and calcium in ordinary meals, there''s no need to plan around it specifically — a normal, varied pattern with regular dairy, dal, grains, and protein sources covers it comfortably. Each day below offers two options for every meal — pick whichever suits you. Phosphorus shows up naturally across most of this week''s choices, so no single meal needs to carry the whole day. If you are managing a health condition - Kidney disease: As kidney function declines, phosphate can build up in the blood, but dietary advice must be individualised. Don''t begin a low-phosphorus diet from this chart alone or remove nutritious foods such as dal, dairy, fish, or meat without guidance. If you have chronic kidney disease or receive dialysis, follow the phosphorus limits, laboratory monitoring, and protein advice given by your renal team. - Thyroid conditions, high blood pressure, previous pre-eclampsia, diabetes, recent gestational diabetes, or PCOS: These don''t require a special restriction on ordinary phosphorus-containing foods. Continue the eating pattern, medication, and monitoring already advised for your condition.',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Phosphorus is the second-most abundant mineral in your body after calcium. Together, they form hydroxyapatite, the main structural material in bones and teeth. Phosphorus is also part of every cell membrane in your body and plays a role in how your cells store and use energy.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya with a glass of milk.", "breakfast_b": "Boiled egg (cooked through) with whole-wheat toast.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a moong dal and a spinach sabzi.", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a beans sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted cashews with a seasonal fruit.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a banana.", "dinner_a": "Paneer-and-vegetable stir-fry with a whole-wheat roti.", "dinner_b": "A curry made with a locally available fish from the lower-mercury category, with rice and a cucumber salad.", "still_hungry": "Whatever''s easiest to reach for right now."}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "A genuine step up while breastfeeding", "notes": "Under ICMR-NIN''s calcium-linked approach, the corresponding phosphorus reference during lactation is about 1,200 mg a day. NIH''s phosphorus RDA remains 700 mg a day during lactation. These are different systems, and ordinary varied meals usually provide phosphorus without deliberate tracking. About your prenatal or postnatal supplement: Phosphorus usually doesn''t need its own supplement because it''s widely present in food. Continue prescribed calcium, prenatal, or postnatal supplements as directed, compare labels before adding anything new, and don''t add phosphorus separately unless your clinician has identified a reason.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with a glass of milk.", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (two eggs, cooked through) with whole-wheat toast.", "lunch_a": "Bajra roti with a rajma curry and a cabbage sabzi.", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with rice and a carrot-and-peas sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with sesame seeds.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted almonds with a seasonal fruit.", "dinner_a": "Vegetable khichdi with a side of curd.", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a tomato salad.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Where phosphorus naturally shows up", "notes": "Because phosphorus accompanies many protein- and calcium-containing foods, today is about noticing the ordinary combinations already present in your meals — not calculating every milligram. A modern source worth knowing about: Some packaged foods and drinks contain phosphate additives used for texture, preservation, or processing. These forms are readily absorbed. For most mothers with healthy kidneys, an occasional packaged food isn''t a reason for concern. If your renal team has asked you to limit phosphate, check ingredient lists for words containing ''phos,'' and ask which local products matter most for you. If you have a diagnosed allergy: If you have a diagnosed egg, dairy, peanut, seed, or seafood allergy, avoid that food completely and choose one of the many other sources in this week''s chart. If a food causes digestive discomfort rather than an allergy, use only forms and portions you already know you tolerate. Don''t trial a problem food to pursue this week''s nutrient.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa with a small bowl of curd.", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with whole-wheat toast.", "lunch_a": "Jowar roti with a chana sabzi and a glass of buttermilk.", "lunch_b": "A curry made with a locally available fish from the lower-mercury category, with rice and a tomato-and-cucumber salad.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of yogurt with a seasonal fruit.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted cashews with a banana.", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti, a masoor dal, and a mushroom sabzi.", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with rice and a cabbage sabzi.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Why more isn''t the goal", "notes": "No upper limit exists for phosphorus in the ICMR-NIN system, and ordinary food isn''t a concern — but concentrated products are a different story. Why more isn''t the goal: Some bowel-cleansing products and laxatives contain sodium phosphate. Taking more than directed, repeating doses too closely, or combining cleansing products can cause severe dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, and kidney injury. Never exceed the label. Ask a clinician or pharmacist before using one if you are pregnant or breastfeeding, have kidney or heart disease, are dehydrated, or take medicines that affect the kidneys or fluid balance. Seek medical help for markedly reduced urination, fainting, confusion, persistent vomiting, inability to keep fluids down, severe abdominal pain, or severe weakness. This isn''t about ordinary laxatives or ordinary meals — most don''t contain phosphate at all, and food-based phosphorus isn''t the concern here.", "breakfast_a": "Oats porridge with a glass of milk.", "breakfast_b": "Chicken sandwich (whole-wheat bread, fully cooked chicken) with a side of tomato.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a rajma curry and a beans sabzi.", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with rice and a green-peas sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "Sunflower seeds with a seasonal fruit.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a seasonal fruit.", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti, a moong dal, and a potato sabzi.", "dinner_b": "A curry made with a locally available fish from the lower-mercury category, with rice and a carrot salad.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "A naturally reassuring nutrient", "notes": "Real phosphorus deficiency from diet alone is genuinely rare, and it''s almost never the result of low food intake. When it happens, it''s usually linked to a separate medical condition, such as a parathyroid problem or certain kidney conditions, not something ordinary eating causes. This isn''t a nutrient that needs active day-to-day tracking.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam with a glass of buttermilk.", "breakfast_b": "Egg paratha (whole-wheat) with a side of curd.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a masoor dal and a cauliflower sabzi.", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a mushroom sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted peanuts with a banana.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with almonds.", "dinner_a": "Sweet potato and vegetable sabzi with a whole-wheat roti.", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with rice and a tomato salad.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Away from home", "notes": "Roasted cashews, sunflower seeds, and roasted chana are naturally easy to carry today — no cold storage needed for these. Food safety: Keep the egg, chicken, and cheese sandwiches, and the flask of milk, refrigerated or in an insulated bag with an ice pack, and make sure they stay cold until you actually eat or drink them — discard anything that''s been sitting out at room temperature for more than about two hours (about one hour in hot weather). Roasted cashews, sunflower seeds, and roasted chana are shelf-stable and don''t need the same cold-chain care. Reheat the dal curry or mutton curry until steaming hot before eating.", "breakfast_a": "A whole-wheat vegetable sandwich with a slice of cheese.", "breakfast_b": "A pre-made boiled-egg sandwich (whole-wheat bread), carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten.", "lunch_a": "A roasted-chana-and-vegetable whole-wheat wrap, carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten.", "lunch_b": "A pre-made chicken sandwich (whole-wheat bread, fully cooked chicken), carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten, with a tomato side.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted cashews, kept portable — no cold storage needed.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small flask of milk, kept cold.", "dinner_a": "A reheated moong dal-and-vegetable curry with rice.", "dinner_b": "A reheated mutton curry with rice and a vegetable side.", "still_hungry": "Whatever''s already within reach."}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "A quiet week to notice what already felt manageable, and what you''d want to carry into the weeks ahead. Still in Rebuilding. A steady, varied pattern of meals does more for you here than any single perfect meal.", "breakfast_a": "Whichever breakfast felt best this week — worth repeating.", "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": "Whichever lunch from this week you''d want again.", "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": "Whichever snack worked best for you this week.", "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": "Whichever dinner from this week you''d want again.", "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": "A familiar snack, whatever suits you."}]'::jsonb,
   'Did anything about how closely phosphorus tracks calcium and protein change how you think about this week''s meals?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another nutrient as you continue rebuilding your own strength.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 25: Potassium, working alongside sodium for steady blood pressure
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   25,
   NULL,
   'Phase 3 of 6 — Rebuilding',
   'Potassium, working alongside sodium for steady blood pressure',
   '"More potassium-rich foods, less excess sodium — unless your care team has given you different limits."',
   'Potassium is an electrolyte your body uses to help regulate fluid balance, nerve signals, and muscle contraction — including your heart''s own rhythm. It also plays a genuine, well-evidenced role in blood pressure: diets richer in potassium and lower in sodium are linked to a real reduction in blood pressure and stroke risk. Your daily reference: ICMR-NIN gives a general adult potassium reference of 3,500 mg a day and a sodium limit/reference of about 2,000 mg a day, equivalent to roughly 5 g of salt from all sources — not 5 g of salt added on top of what''s already in food. It also considers their relationship using a desirable sodium-to-potassium ratio expressed in molar units, not equal milligrams. The practical message is simpler than the maths: include potassium-rich whole foods regularly while avoiding excessive sodium from added salt, packaged foods, and restaurant meals. No separate pregnancy or lactation figure is published for potassium — the same reference applies at every stage. For women aged 19 years and older, NIH''s US Adequate Intake is 2,600 mg a day when non-pregnant, 2,900 mg a day during pregnancy, and 2,800 mg a day while breastfeeding — slightly lower than pregnancy, a genuine feature of the NIH system, not an error. If you are under 19, use the age-specific guidance given by your maternity team. These are two different reference systems, presented honestly rather than reconciled into one number. If you are breastfeeding or expressing milk: If you''re breastfeeding, NIH''s reference is 2,800 mg a day — slightly below the pregnancy figure. ICMR-NIN doesn''t publish a separate breastfeeding figure, so its general 3,500 mg a day reference applies. A varied day containing legumes, vegetables, fruit, and dairy or other protein foods can contribute substantially without requiring exact counting. If you are not breastfeeding: If you''re not breastfeeding, potassium remains useful for everyday fluid balance and heart rhythm, and your daily reference returns to the general adult figure. Where potassium comes from: Potassium is genuinely abundant across ordinary staples — legumes and dal, potato, curd, spinach, tomato, and a wide range of fruit (orange, mosambi, guava, papaya, banana, and others) all contribute meaningfully. Even in places where this has been studied directly, most people eat somewhat less potassium than the reference suggests, so leaning toward a variety of these foods regularly is genuinely useful, not just a formality. A gentle note for this week: No upper limit has been set for potassium from food in either reference system, for someone with healthy kidneys — the body can usually regulate potassium from ordinary foods when kidney function is normal. The real caution is for anyone with reduced kidney function, or taking certain medicines (see the condition note below), where even ordinary potassium intake can build up. Potassium supplements can cause harm in the wrong dose or medical context — don''t add one without your clinician''s guidance. This week''s rhythm: Because potassium is spread naturally across so many ordinary foods, there''s no need to plan around it specifically — a normal, varied pattern with legumes, vegetables, fruit, and dairy or other protein foods contributes substantially, without needing exact counting. Each day below offers two options for every meal — pick whichever suits you. Potassium shows up naturally across most of this week''s choices, so no single meal needs to carry the whole day. If you are managing a health condition - Kidney disease, or taking a medicine that can raise potassium: If you have chronic kidney disease, receive dialysis, have previously had high blood potassium, or take medicine that can raise potassium, follow your own laboratory monitoring and food guidance rather than this general chart. High potassium can sometimes cause no noticeable symptoms, so prescribed blood tests matter. - High blood pressure or previous pre-eclampsia: Potassium-rich whole foods and avoiding excess sodium can support a blood-pressure-friendly eating pattern, but they don''t treat postpartum hypertension or replace medicine and monitoring. Seek urgent assessment for severe or persistent headache, visual changes, chest pain, breathlessness, severe upper-abdominal pain, sudden marked swelling, or a blood-pressure reading at or above the urgent threshold your maternity team gave you. - Thyroid conditions, diabetes, recent gestational diabetes, or PCOS: These don''t require a special restriction on ordinary potassium-containing foods. Continue the eating pattern, medication, and monitoring already advised for your condition. Choose whole fruit more often than juice, and keep potato, fruit, rice, and roti portions consistent with the guidance already given to you.',
