   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Potassium is an electrolyte your body uses to help regulate fluid balance, nerve signals, and muscle contraction — including your heart''s own rhythm. It also plays a genuine, well-evidenced role in blood pressure: diets richer in potassium and lower in sodium are linked to a real reduction in blood pressure and stroke risk.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with a whole orange, mosambi, guava, or another seasonal whole fruit.", "breakfast_b": "Boiled egg (cooked through) with whole-wheat toast and a banana.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a rajma curry and a spinach sabzi.", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a potato sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted peanuts with a guava.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a seasonal fruit.", "dinner_a": "Sweet potato and vegetable sabzi with a whole-wheat roti.", "dinner_b": "A curry made with a locally available fish from the lower-mercury category, with rice and a tomato salad.", "still_hungry": "Whatever''s easiest to reach for right now."}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "A gentle shift after pregnancy", "notes": "NIH''s reference for potassium actually eases slightly after pregnancy — 2,900 mg a day in pregnancy, 2,800 mg a day while breastfeeding. ICMR-NIN''s general 3,500 mg a day reference doesn''t change by stage. A varied day containing legumes, vegetables, fruit, and dairy or other protein foods can contribute substantially without requiring exact counting. About your prenatal or postnatal supplement: Potassium supplements can cause harm in the wrong dose or medical context. Check prenatal, postnatal, electrolyte, effervescent, sports, and recovery products for potassium, and don''t add a potassium-only product without clinical advice.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa with a small bowl of curd.", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (two eggs, cooked through) with whole-wheat toast.", "lunch_a": "Bajra roti with a moong dal and a beans sabzi.", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with rice and a tomato-and-cucumber salad.", "nourishment_break_a": "An orange with roasted almonds.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with sunflower seeds.", "dinner_a": "Vegetable khichdi with a side of curd.", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a cabbage sabzi.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Where potassium naturally shows up", "notes": "Potassium appears across legumes, vegetables, dairy or tolerated alternatives, and many whole fruits. Today is about rotating these ordinary foods rather than relying on bananas or counting every milligram. If you have a diagnosed allergy: If you have a diagnosed egg, dairy, peanut, seed, or seafood allergy, avoid that food completely and choose one of the many other sources in this week''s chart. If a food causes digestive discomfort rather than an allergy, use only forms and portions you already know you tolerate. Don''t trial a problem food to pursue this week''s nutrient.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable daliya with a banana.", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with whole-wheat toast.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a chana sabzi and a small bowl of curd.", "lunch_b": "A curry made with a locally available fish from the lower-mercury category, with rice and a cucumber salad.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted cashews with a papaya.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of yogurt with a seasonal fruit.", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti, a masoor dal, and a mushroom sabzi.", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with rice and a cabbage sabzi.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Why more isn''t the goal", "notes": "No upper limit exists for potassium from food in a healthy adult — but medicines and salt substitutes change the picture. Why more isn''t the goal: Some medicines — including ACE inhibitors, ARBs, and potassium-sparing diuretics — can raise blood potassium, especially when kidney function is reduced. This isn''t a complete medication list. Don''t stop prescribed medicine or independently restrict nutritious foods. Ask your clinician or pharmacist before adding potassium supplements, electrolyte powders, or potassium-based salt substitutes. About potassium-based salt substitutes: Some low-sodium, ''lite,'' or mineral salts replace part of the sodium with potassium chloride. Don''t switch to a potassium-based salt substitute solely for this week''s theme. Check with your clinician or pharmacist first if you have kidney or heart disease, diabetes with kidney involvement, or take medicine that may raise potassium.", "breakfast_a": "Oats porridge with a mosambi.", "breakfast_b": "Chicken sandwich (whole-wheat bread, fully cooked chicken) with a side of tomato.", "lunch_a": "Jowar roti with a rajma curry and a beans sabzi.", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with rice and a green-peas sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "Sunflower seeds with an orange.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with almonds.", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti, a moong dal, and a potato sabzi.", "dinner_b": "A curry made with a locally available fish from the lower-mercury category, with rice and a carrot salad.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "A naturally reassuring nutrient", "notes": "Low potassium is rarely caused by one imperfect food day. It''s more often associated with prolonged vomiting or diarrhea, certain medicines, or laxative overuse. Symptoms such as fatigue, constipation, or muscle weakness are nonspecific, so don''t diagnose or treat yourself with potassium supplements. Follow your clinician''s rehydration advice, and seek prompt care for persistent vomiting or diarrhea, inability to keep fluids down, markedly reduced urination, palpitations, fainting, confusion, chest symptoms, or severe or worsening weakness.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam with a small bowl of curd.", "breakfast_b": "Egg paratha (whole-wheat) with a side of curd.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a masoor dal and a potato sabzi.", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a mushroom sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted chana with a banana.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of yogurt with sunflower seeds.", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti, a moong dal, and a cauliflower sabzi.", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with rice and a tomato salad.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Away from home", "notes": "Roasted cashews, sunflower seeds, and roasted chana are naturally easy to carry today — no cold storage needed for these. Food safety: Keep the egg, chicken, and cheese sandwiches, and the flask of buttermilk, refrigerated or in an insulated bag with an ice pack, and make sure they stay cold until you actually eat or drink them — discard anything that''s been sitting out at room temperature for more than about two hours (about one hour in hot weather). Roasted cashews, sunflower seeds, and roasted chana are shelf-stable and don''t need the same cold-chain care. Reheat the dal curry or mutton curry until steaming hot before eating.", "breakfast_a": "A whole-wheat vegetable sandwich with a slice of cheese.", "breakfast_b": "A pre-made boiled-egg sandwich (whole-wheat bread), carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten.", "lunch_a": "A roasted-chana-and-vegetable whole-wheat wrap, carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten.", "lunch_b": "A pre-made chicken sandwich (whole-wheat bread, fully cooked chicken), carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten, with a tomato side.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted cashews, kept portable — no cold storage needed.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small flask of buttermilk, kept cold.", "dinner_a": "A reheated moong dal-and-vegetable curry with rice.", "dinner_b": "A reheated mutton curry with rice and a vegetable side.", "still_hungry": "Whatever''s already within reach."}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "A quiet week to notice what already felt manageable, and what you''d want to carry into the weeks ahead. Still in Rebuilding. A steady, varied pattern of meals does more for you here than any single perfect meal.", "breakfast_a": "Whichever breakfast felt best this week — worth repeating.", "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": "Whichever lunch from this week you''d want again.", "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": "Whichever snack worked best for you this week.", "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": "Whichever dinner from this week you''d want again.", "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": "A familiar snack, whatever suits you."}]'::jsonb,
   'Did learning about the sodium-potassium pairing change how you think about the salt and fruit already in your day?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another nutrient as you continue rebuilding your own strength.')
