-- postpartum week 30: Molybdenum, a small mineral your ordinary meals already cover
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   30,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'Molybdenum, a small mineral your ordinary meals already cover',
   '"A small mineral your everyday meals already take care of."',
   'Molybdenum helps a small group of enzymes process sulfur-containing amino acids and other compounds. Your body needs only a tiny amount, and ordinary varied diets generally provide enough. Reference amount: ICMR-NIN''s 2020 report doesn''t include a molybdenum figure — that reflects this particular report, not a sign that Indian nutrition science has overlooked the nutrient. NIH''s US reference is used here instead. For women aged 19 and older, NIH''s Recommended Dietary Allowance is 45 mcg a day when non-pregnant, rising to 50 mcg a day during both pregnancy and lactation. For ages 14–18, the figures are 43 mcg a day non-pregnant, and also 50 mcg during pregnancy and lactation. Where it naturally shows up: Molybdenum''s richest everyday sources are ordinary and plant-forward — nothing exotic or difficult to find. Legumes lead the way: rajma, chana, lobia, moong, masoor, and mixed dal all contribute. Whole grains, milk and dairy, and peanuts add smaller amounts. Liver is also a source, but is deliberately left out of this week''s chart for the same reason explained in Week 17 on vitamin A. Ordinary meat, poultry, and fish contribute comparatively little — worth knowing if you''ve been assuming a protein-heavy day automatically covers this nutrient too. The amount in any particular food also varies with the soil and water it was grown in, so this is a week about a general eating pattern, not about totting up exact micrograms from individual dishes. The gentle part: Deficiency is extraordinarily rare in people eating an ordinary diet, and no generally healthy population group is known to be routinely at risk. The real takeaway: Regular legumes and varied staple foods quietly cover many nutritional needs — including trace minerals like this one that you don''t need to track individually. Each day below offers two options for every meal — pick whichever suits you. Molybdenum shows up naturally across many of this week''s choices, so no single meal needs to carry the whole day. If you are managing a health condition - Thyroid conditions, high blood pressure, previous pre-eclampsia, diabetes, recent gestational diabetes, or PCOS: These don''t require a special restriction on ordinary molybdenum-containing foods. Continue the eating pattern, medication, and monitoring already advised for your condition.',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "You don''t need to track this one — a normal week of legumes, grains, and varied plates already covers it. Today''s meals show what that looks like in practice.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal chilla with a small bowl of curd.", "breakfast_b": "Egg curry (cooked through) with whole-wheat roti and a green-peas side.", "lunch_a": "Lobia (black-eyed peas) vegetable pulao with a cucumber raita.", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with a masoor dal and rice.", "nourishment_break_a": "Kala chana chaat with a squeeze of lemon.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a seasonal fruit.", "dinner_a": "Dal dhokli (mixed dal with whole-wheat dumplings).", "dinner_b": "A curry made with a locally available fish from the lower-mercury category, with a small chana side and rice.", "still_hungry": "Whatever''s easiest to reach for right now."}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "A small step up while breastfeeding", "notes": "If you are breastfeeding or expressing milk, NIH''s reference is 50 mcg a day — the same figure used during pregnancy. If you are not breastfeeding, the adult reference is 45 mcg a day for ages 19 and older. The difference is small and does not require tracking individual foods.", "breakfast_a": "Besan (chickpea flour) vegetable chilla with a seasonal fruit.", "breakfast_b": "Masala scrambled eggs with a small bowl of curd.", "lunch_a": "Mixed-dal dosa with a coconut chutney.", "lunch_b": "Mutton keema (cooked through) with a green-peas curry and rice.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted peanuts with a banana.", "nourishment_break_b": "Whole-wheat crackers with a small bowl of curd.", "dinner_a": "Rajma vegetable bowl with brown rice.", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with a moong dal and whole-wheat roti.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Where molybdenum naturally shows up", "notes": "Legumes are the richest everyday sources, while grains, dairy, peanuts and other varied foods contribute too. The amount naturally varies with growing conditions, so think in terms of a varied pattern rather than exact numbers. If you have a food allergy: If you have a diagnosed egg, dairy, peanut, tree-nut, seed, fish, or shellfish allergy, avoid that food completely and choose a tolerated alternative from this week''s chart. If a food causes digestive discomfort rather than an allergy, use only forms and portions you already know you tolerate. Do not trial a problem food to pursue this week''s nutrient.", "breakfast_a": "Savoury oats with green peas and a small bowl of curd.", "breakfast_b": "A boiled-egg-and-vegetable salad with whole-wheat bread.", "lunch_a": "Chana-and-vegetable tikki with whole-wheat roti, cucumber salad and a small bowl of curd.", "lunch_b": "Grilled or pan-cooked lower-mercury fish (cooked through) with a lobia side and rice.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small sattu drink made with reliably safe water, or roasted chana with fruit.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of yogurt with roasted peanuts.", "dinner_a": "Moong-millet pulao with a mixed-vegetable raita.", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a green-peas side.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Why more isn''t the goal", "notes": "More isn''t the goal here — there''s a real upper limit, but reaching it through food alone is genuinely unusual. Worth knowing: A real Tolerable Upper Limit does exist: NIH''s upper limit is 2,000 mcg a day for adults aged 19 and older and 1,700 mcg a day for ages 14–18, including pregnancy and lactation. Harm from food is extremely unusual — one report involved exceptionally high dietary exposure in a region with unusually molybdenum-rich soil, and industrial exposure has also caused problems. These situations are far removed from an ordinary varied diet. About supplements: There is usually no reason to seek a separate molybdenum supplement. Continue any prenatal or multivitamin recommended by your care team, and check before adding a stand-alone mineral product.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat) with roasted peanuts.", "breakfast_b": "Chicken sandwich (whole-wheat bread, fully cooked chicken) with a cucumber side.", "lunch_a": "Kala chana curry with jeera rice and a cucumber salad.", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with a masoor dal and rice.", "nourishment_break_a": "A boiled potato chaat with roasted peanuts.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with almonds.", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a mixed-dal and a bottle-gourd sabzi.", "dinner_b": "A curry made with a locally available fish from the lower-mercury category, with a small masoor dal side and rice.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "A naturally reassuring nutrient", "notes": "Deficiency is extraordinarily rare in people eating an ordinary diet, and no generally healthy population group is known to be routinely at risk. Regular legumes and varied staple foods quietly cover many nutritional needs — including trace minerals like this one that you don''t need to track individually.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi (finger millet) dosa with a tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd.", "breakfast_b": "Egg-stuffed whole-wheat paratha with a side of curd.", "lunch_a": "Masoor dal with jeera rice and a cucumber salad.", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with a rajma side and rice.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with a seasonal fruit.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted peanuts with a slice of papaya.", "dinner_a": "Kala chana curry with brown rice.", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with a bottle-gourd sabzi and rice.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Away from home", "notes": "A day built around things that travel well — dry, portable staples rather than anything that needs assembling on the go. Food safety: Keep the boiled egg, chicken-and-dal roll, hummus-style chana wrap and buttermilk refrigerated or in an insulated bag with an ice pack until eaten. If the sattu drink is mixed before leaving home, carry it cold and drink it within a few hours; alternatively, carry the dry sattu separately and mix it with reliably safe water when ready to drink. Only the dry roasted-poha-and-chana mixture and unmixed sattu powder are shelf-stable. Discard perishable food left at room temperature for more than about two hours, or about one hour in hot weather. Reheat dinner until steaming hot.", "breakfast_a": "A dry roasted-poha-and-chana mixture, prepared like a simple homemade chivda.", "breakfast_b": "A hard-boiled egg (cooked through) with a small whole-wheat bun, carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten.", "lunch_a": "A hummus-style chana spread in a whole-wheat wrap, carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten.", "lunch_b": "A pre-made chicken-and-dal filled whole-wheat paratha roll, carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten.", "nourishment_break_a": "A sattu drink (mixed with water and a pinch of salt or jaggery), kept in a flask.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small flask of spiced buttermilk (chaas), kept cold.", "dinner_a": "A reheated dal dhokli.", "dinner_b": "A reheated mutton keema curry with a carrot sabzi and rice.", "still_hungry": "Whatever''s already within reach."}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "A quiet week to notice what already felt manageable, and what you''d want to carry into the weeks ahead. Still in Settling into strength. A steady, varied pattern of meals does more for you here than any single perfect meal — and that''s especially true for a nutrient like this one, which you were never meant to track dish by dish.", "breakfast_a": "Whichever breakfast felt best this week — worth repeating.", "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": "Whichever lunch from this week you''d want again.", "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": "Whichever snack worked best for you this week.", "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": "Whichever dinner from this week you''d want again.", "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": "A familiar snack, whatever suits you."}]'::jsonb,
   'This was one of the calmer nutrients in this series — did noticing that change how much mental effort you put into planning this week''s meals, compared to others?',
   'Next week brings another everyday nutrient your body relies on, found in foods you likely already eat.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 31: Sodium, the mineral you almost never need to seek out
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   31,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'Sodium, the mineral you almost never need to seek out',
   '"Already in nearly everything you cook — this week is about noticing the pattern, not measuring every grain."',
   'Sodium, along with chloride (its partner in ordinary table salt), helps maintain fluid balance, nerve signals, and muscle function, including your heart''s own rhythm. Almost every Indian kitchen already uses salt generously in daily cooking, which makes this one of the few nutrients in this series where the real story is moderation, not pursuit. Reference amount: ICMR-NIN recommends limiting total salt to about 5 g a day, equivalent to approximately 2,000 mg sodium. This includes salt used during cooking and at the table, as well as sodium from packaged and prepared foods. It is a population-level upper goal, not an amount to deliberately reach. The US National Academies give an Adequate Intake of 1,500 mg sodium a day for adult women and recommend reducing intake when it is above 2,300 mg a day to lower chronic-disease risk. These are two different reference systems, presented honestly rather than reconciled into one number. If you are breastfeeding: Breastfeeding does not increase the established sodium intake reference. Your body regulates sodium in breast milk, so eating extra salt is not recommended as a way to support milk supply. ICMR-NIN publishes no separate pregnancy or lactation figure either. Where it naturally shows up: For many Indian households, sodium comes from both the salt added during cooking and concentrated sources such as pickles, papad, packaged snacks, instant foods, sauces and restaurant meals. The useful habit is to notice the total pattern and reduce excess gradually — not to make food tasteless or measure every grain of salt. The gentle part: Ordinary diets generally provide sufficient sodium. A diagnosed low blood-sodium level is usually related to fluid balance, illness, medicines, or another medical cause — it is not something to correct by eating extra salt or drinking ORS without medical advice. The real takeaway: Ordinary diets generally provide sufficient sodium. The practical question is whether salt from cooking, table additions, packaged foods and meals outside the home is adding up beyond what is needed. A note on iodized salt: Use iodized salt for ordinary cooking, but use it thoughtfully. Reducing excess salt does not mean switching to uniodized specialty salt — the iodine guidance from Week 10 still applies. A note on salt substitutes: Some "low-sodium" or "lite" salts replace part of the sodium with potassium chloride. These aren''t automatically a better swap for everyone — they can be unsuitable for people with kidney disease or certain medicines. Check with your clinician before switching, rather than assuming a substitute is safer by default. This week isn''t about choosing "more" or "less" sodium foods — normal, freshly cooked meals already contain what you need. Each day below still offers two options for every meal, same as any other week — pick whichever suits you. If you are managing a health condition - High blood pressure, previous pre-eclampsia, kidney disease, or heart failure: These are the situations where sodium genuinely deserves closer attention. Follow the specific sodium guidance your care team has given you rather than general population-level advice — your individual limit may be stricter than the reference used in this chart, and moderation habits that are optional for most mothers may be part of your actual treatment plan. Do not switch to a potassium-containing salt substitute unless your clinician confirms it is appropriate for you. - Thyroid conditions, diabetes, recent gestational diabetes, or PCOS: These don''t require a special sodium restriction beyond the general moderation already described this week. Continue the eating pattern, medication, and monitoring already advised for your condition.',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "This week isn''t about avoiding salt — it''s about noticing where it adds up across a normal day, at home and outside it.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam with a lightly salted homemade tomato chutney.", "breakfast_b": "Chicken sandwich made with fully cooked chicken and whole-wheat bread — compare bread labels where practical, and skip an extra salty accompaniment.", "lunch_a": "Lemon rice with a moong dal and a cucumber salad.", "lunch_b": "A curry made with a locally available fish from the lower-mercury category, with rice and a carrot sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "Fresh fruit with a small handful of unsalted nuts.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a seasonal fruit.", "dinner_a": "Vegetable handvo with a side of curd.", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with roti and a cabbage sabzi.", "still_hungry": "Whatever''s easiest to reach for right now."}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "A reference that doesn''t rise while breastfeeding", "notes": "Breastfeeding doesn''t raise this reference at all — a genuine exception, since most nutrients in this series go up while breastfeeding. See \"Reference amount\" above for the exact figures.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable sevai with unsalted roasted peanuts.", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable pulao.", "lunch_a": "A substantial fresh corn-and-chana bowl with cucumber, tomato and lemon, with a small bowl of curd.", "lunch_b": "Fish tikka (cooked through) with roti and a mixed-vegetable sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with unsalted almonds.", "nourishment_break_b": "Homemade makhana mixture (modest salt) with a seasonal fruit.", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a rajma curry and a bottle-gourd sabzi.", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a beans sabzi.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Where sodium quietly shows up", "notes": "A few practical habits go further than any single rule: reduce cooking salt gradually so your taste can adapt, taste food before adding more at the table, lean on lemon, herbs, garlic, ginger, and spices for flavour, choose unsalted nuts over salted ones, keep papad, pickle, and salty chutneys occasional or in modest portions, compare sodium on labels when buying bread, crackers, sauces, or packaged snacks, and avoid stacking several salty accompaniments onto one meal. If your household is curious about its cooking-salt pattern, noticing how long a packet lasts can provide a rough starting point. It is not a personal intake calculation, especially when household size and meals eaten outside vary. Make any reduction gradual and shared across the household. If you have a food allergy: If you have a diagnosed egg, dairy, peanut, tree-nut, seed, fish, or shellfish allergy, avoid that food completely and choose a tolerated alternative from this week''s chart. If a food causes digestive discomfort rather than an allergy, use only forms and portions you already know you tolerate. Do not trial a problem food to pursue this week''s nutrient.", "breakfast_a": "Methi paratha with a small bowl of curd.", "breakfast_b": "A boiled egg (cooked through) with whole-wheat toast — a modest, unsalted-butter spread.", "lunch_a": "Paneer bhurji with phulka and a cucumber salad.", "lunch_b": "Home-cooked chicken stew (no stock cubes) with rice and a mixed-vegetable sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of yogurt with a seasonal fruit.", "nourishment_break_b": "A modest portion of whole-wheat crackers with unsalted peanuts.", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a chana curry and a potato sabzi.", "dinner_b": "A curry made with a locally available fish from the lower-mercury category, with rice and a green-peas sabzi.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Why moderation, not more, is the real message", "notes": "ICMR-NIN''s population goal of about 2,000 mg a day (roughly 5 g of salt) exists because diets that regularly run well above it are linked, over time, to higher blood pressure and greater cardiovascular and stroke risk — not because any single salty meal causes harm. The US National Academies'' separate Chronic Disease Risk Reduction Intake of 2,300 mg a day reflects the same idea from a different reference system. One salty meal isn''t a failure — the pattern across days and weeks is what matters. Use iodized salt for ordinary cooking, but use it thoughtfully. Reducing excess salt does not mean switching to uniodized specialty salt — the iodine guidance from Week 10 still applies. Some \"low-sodium\" or \"lite\" salts replace part of the sodium with potassium chloride. These aren''t automatically a better swap for everyone — they can be unsuitable for people with kidney disease or certain medicines. Check with your clinician before switching, rather than assuming a substitute is safer by default.", "breakfast_a": "Idli with a coconut chutney (modest salt).", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (two eggs, cooked through) with whole-wheat toast.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a lobia curry and a cauliflower sabzi.", "lunch_b": "Mutton keema (cooked through) with whole-wheat roti and a green-peas sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "A steamed sprouted-moong chaat with a squeeze of lemon.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a banana.", "dinner_a": "Brown rice with a masoor dal and a bottle-gourd sabzi.", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a bhindi sabzi.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "When fluid and electrolyte losses occur", "notes": "Repeated diarrhoea or vomiting can cause dehydration and electrolyte loss. A pharmacy ORS can help replace water and electrolytes together when it is mixed exactly according to the packet directions. Do not make it stronger, add extra salt, or use it as an everyday drink. Heavy sweating on an ordinary hot day can usually be managed with safe water, regular meals and rest; prolonged or extreme heat exposure may need individual advice. Continue breastfeeding or expressing if you feel able, but prioritise your own hydration and seek help if illness is making feeding or caring for your baby difficult. Get prompt medical advice if: you cannot keep fluids down, have blood in the stool, feel faint, become unusually drowsy or confused, develop a severe headache, or urinate much less than usual.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma with a small bowl of curd.", "breakfast_b": "Egg curry (cooked through) with roti and a green-peas side.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a masoor dal and a potato sabzi.", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a green-peas sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with a seasonal fruit.", "nourishment_break_b": "Fresh fruit with unsalted roasted peanuts.", "dinner_a": "Khichdi (moong dal and rice) with a mixed-vegetable sabzi.", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with rice and a carrot sabzi.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Away from home", "notes": "Away from home is where concentrated sources add up fastest — a little awareness goes further here than any special avoidance. Food safety: Keep the boiled egg, chicken sandwich, hummus-style wrap and buttermilk refrigerated or in an insulated bag with an ice pack until eaten, and discard anything left at room temperature for more than about two hours, or about one hour in hot weather. The unsalted nuts and fruit are shelf-stable and don''t need the same care. Reheat dinner until steaming hot.", "breakfast_a": "A whole-wheat vegetable sandwich with a modest slice of cheese.", "breakfast_b": "A hard-boiled egg (cooked through) with a small whole-wheat bun, carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten.", "lunch_a": "A homemade hummus-style chana spread (no packaged sauce) in a whole-wheat wrap, carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten.", "lunch_b": "A pre-made chicken sandwich (whole-wheat bread, fully cooked chicken), carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten, with a cucumber side.", "nourishment_break_a": "Fresh fruit with a small pack of unsalted nuts, kept portable — no cold storage needed.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small flask of buttermilk, kept cold.", "dinner_a": "A reheated rajma curry with rice.", "dinner_b": "A reheated mutton curry with rice and a mixed-vegetable sabzi.", "still_hungry": "Whatever''s already within reach."}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "A quiet week to notice what already felt manageable, and what you''d want to carry into the weeks ahead. Still in Settling into strength. This week''s real message is one of the simplest in this series: notice the total pattern across cooking, table, and packaged food, and let any change happen gradually. One salty meal was never the point.", "breakfast_a": "Whichever breakfast felt best this week — worth repeating.", "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": "Whichever lunch from this week you''d want again.", "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": "Whichever snack worked best for you this week.", "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": "Whichever dinner from this week you''d want again.", "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": "A familiar snack, whatever suits you."}]'::jsonb,
   'This was one of the few weeks about noticing what''s already there rather than adding something new — did that change how you thought about the salt in your cooking, not just packaged food?',
   'Next week brings another everyday nutrient your body relies on, found in foods you likely already eat.')
