-- postpartum week 32: Water, the nutrient this series almost forgot
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   32,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'Water, the nutrient this series almost forgot',
   '"One of the easiest nutrients to actually meet — this week is about noticing it, not measuring it."',
   'Water carries nutrients to your cells, removes waste, regulates your body temperature, and is the single largest ingredient in breast milk itself — about 87% water. It''s the one thing this series has never given its own week. Reference amount: The US National Academies give Adequate Intakes for total water of 2.7 L a day for non-pregnant adult women, 3.0 L during pregnancy and 3.8 L during lactation. These are total-water figures, not plain-water prescriptions. Water from food, plain water, and other beverages all contributes. In the underlying US data, approximately 3.1 L of the lactation total came from water and other beverages, with the remainder coming from food. ICMR-NIN''s Dietary Guidelines for Indians (2024) advises pregnant and breastfeeding mothers to drink over 2 litres of fluid a day, including water and other beverages — broad guidance covering both stages together, not a personalised prescription. These are two different reference systems, presented honestly rather than reconciled into one number. An Adequate Intake is a population reference, not a precise personal requirement. Your needs can vary with climate, activity, illness, diet and individual health guidance. Where it naturally shows up: Roughly a fifth of your daily fluid comes from food itself — watery fruits and vegetables, dal, curries, and soups all count. Tea and coffee contribute to fluid intake at ordinary amounts; they do not need to be "cancelled out" with extra water. If breastfeeding, keep total caffeine under 200 mg a day unless advised otherwise. The gentle part: On an ordinary day, thirst and drinking with meals are useful guides for most healthy adults. Hot weather, prolonged activity, fever, vomiting, diarrhoea, and some medical conditions can change what you need. The real takeaway: For most mothers, drinking through the day rather than in occasional large amounts is what actually works — not a fixed glass count. Each day below still offers two options for every meal, same as any other week — pick whichever suits you. This week isn''t about choosing "hydrating" foods — it''s about simple habits like keeping water accessible and drinking with meals, which matter more than any single dish. If you are managing a health condition - Kidney disease or heart failure: These are situations where your care team may have given you a specific fluid limit, since these conditions can affect how your body clears water. Follow their guidance rather than the general reference used in this chart if the two differ. - High blood pressure, thyroid conditions, diabetes, recent gestational diabetes, or PCOS: These don''t require a special fluid restriction. High blood pressure specifically is a sodium-related caution (see Week 31), not a water-intake one, and shouldn''t be treated as a reason to drink less. Continue the eating and drinking pattern, medication, and monitoring already advised for your condition.',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "This week''s nutrient isn''t a food at all — it''s water. Simple, but genuinely the one this series has never given its own week. A simple habit worth starting this week: keep a filled bottle or glass of water wherever you usually feed your baby, so it''s there when thirst catches up with you.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable oats chilla with a bowl of curd.", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with whole-wheat toast.", "lunch_a": "Curd rice with mixed cooked vegetables and a cucumber salad.", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a tomato-cucumber salad.", "nourishment_break_a": "Watermelon or muskmelon, whichever is in season, with a small handful of roasted chana or tolerated unsalted nuts.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a seasonal fruit.", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a moong dal and a bottle-gourd sabzi.", "dinner_b": "A curry made with a locally available fish from the lower-mercury category, with rice and a carrot sabzi.", "still_hungry": "Whatever''s easiest to reach for right now."}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Why breastfeeding changes the reference", "notes": "If you are breastfeeding or expressing milk: The US National Academies'' total-water reference is 3.8 L a day during lactation, compared with 2.7 L for a non-pregnant adult woman. This total includes water from food and all beverages; it is not a prescription to drink 3.8 L of plain water. Drinking beyond thirst has not been shown to create extra milk. If milk supply feels low, feeding frequency, effective milk removal, attachment, and the baby''s growth or wet nappies are more useful things to assess than forcing additional water. Illness or significant dehydration still deserves attention.", "breakfast_a": "Appam with a light vegetable stew.", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (two eggs, cooked through) with whole-wheat toast.", "lunch_a": "Vegetable daliya pulao with a cucumber raita.", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with roti and a green-peas sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "A glass of homemade lassi (lightly sweetened or unsweetened) with roasted peanuts.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with almonds.", "dinner_a": "Khichdi (moong dal and rice) with a mixed-vegetable soup on the side.", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a spinach sabzi.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Where your fluid actually comes from", "notes": "Roughly a fifth of your daily fluid comes from food itself — watery fruits and vegetables, dal, curries, and soups all count. Tea and coffee contribute to fluid intake at ordinary amounts; they do not need to be \"cancelled out\" with extra water. If breastfeeding, keep total caffeine under 200 mg a day unless advised otherwise. If you have a food allergy: If you have a diagnosed egg, dairy, peanut, tree-nut, seed, fish, or shellfish allergy, avoid that food completely and choose a tolerated alternative from this week''s chart. If a food causes digestive discomfort rather than an allergy, use only forms and portions you already know you tolerate. Do not trial a problem food to pursue this week''s nutrient.", "breakfast_a": "Fully cooked moong-sprouts cheela with freshly prepared mint chutney.", "breakfast_b": "A boiled egg (cooked through) with whole-wheat toast.", "lunch_a": "Vegetable pulao with a cucumber-tomato raita.", "lunch_b": "A curry made with a locally available fish from the lower-mercury category, with rice and a bhindi sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "A cup of tea or coffee, within the caffeine limit, with roasted chana or a small homemade snack.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of yogurt with a seasonal fruit.", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a chana curry and a tomato-cucumber salad.", "dinner_b": "Chicken stew (cooked through, no stock cubes) with rice and a cabbage sabzi.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Drinking to thirst, and when more isn''t the goal", "notes": "Drinking excessive amounts very quickly can dilute blood sodium, although this is rare in ordinary daily life. Risk can be higher during prolonged exercise, with certain illnesses or medicines, or when the body cannot clear water normally. Do not force large volumes beyond thirst, and follow any clinician-prescribed fluid plan.", "breakfast_a": "Paneer-and-vegetable stuffed paratha with a bowl of curd.", "breakfast_b": "Chicken bhurji (cooked through) with whole-wheat toast.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a rajma curry and a cucumber salad.", "lunch_b": "Mutton keema (cooked through) with whole-wheat roti and a carrot sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted makhana with a small bowl of curd.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted chana with a seasonal fruit.", "dinner_a": "Brown rice with a masoor dal and a bottle-gourd sabzi.", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (cooked through) with rice and a beans sabzi.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Signs it''s time to drink more", "notes": "Urine colour can be a rough clue: consistently dark yellow urine together with thirst or dry mouth may mean you need more fluid. First-morning urine is often darker, and vitamins, medicines, and foods can also change the colour — so it is not a diagnostic test. In hot or humid weather, keep safe water accessible and drink regularly rather than ignoring thirst for long periods. More may be needed when sweating is prolonged, but there is no universal extra-litre rule. Get prompt medical advice if: you cannot keep fluids down, have repeated vomiting or diarrhoea, feel faint, become confused or unusually drowsy, develop a fast heartbeat that does not settle, or pass much less urine than usual.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat porridge) with a small bowl of curd.", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable pulao.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a lobia curry and a bottle-gourd sabzi.", "lunch_b": "A curry made with a locally available fish from the lower-mercury category, with rice and a green-peas sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "A glass of buttermilk with a seasonal fruit.", "nourishment_break_b": "Fresh fruit with roasted peanuts.", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a moong dal and a carrot sabzi.", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with rice and a cabbage sabzi.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Away from home", "notes": "Choose water from a source you reasonably trust — such as reliably treated water, a properly sealed bottle from a reputable source, or water boiled and cooled safely. Avoid ice when its source is uncertain. About the coconut water: Coconut water is an optional drink, not a hydration requirement. Choose an unsweetened portion if it suits you. If you have kidney disease, a potassium restriction, or diabetes requiring carbohydrate monitoring, use the beverage plan advised for you. Food safety: Keep the egg wrap, chicken sandwich, paneer wrap and any dairy drink refrigerated or in an insulated bag with an ice pack until eaten, and discard anything left at room temperature for more than about two hours, or about one hour in hot weather. Check a packaged coconut water''s own label for storage guidance — once opened, keep it cold and use it promptly. The unsalted nuts and fruit are shelf-stable and don''t need the same care. Reheat dinner until steaming hot.", "breakfast_a": "A whole-wheat vegetable sandwich, carried with a small bottle of safe drinking water.", "breakfast_b": "An egg-and-vegetable wrap (fully cooked egg, whole-wheat wrap), carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten.", "lunch_a": "A vegetable-and-paneer whole-wheat wrap, carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten.", "lunch_b": "A pre-made chicken sandwich (whole-wheat bread, fully cooked chicken), carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten, with a cucumber side.", "nourishment_break_a": "Fresh fruit with unsalted nuts, kept portable — no cold storage needed.", "nourishment_break_b": "A sealed, packaged coconut water, or a small flask of homemade lassi, kept cold.", "dinner_a": "A reheated rajma curry with rice.", "dinner_b": "A reheated mutton curry with rice and a potato sabzi.", "still_hungry": "Whatever''s already within reach."}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "A quiet week to notice what already felt manageable, and what you''d want to carry into the weeks ahead. Still in Settling into strength. Something as ordinary as accessible safe water can quietly support you through feeding, recovery and hot days — without measuring every glass or forcing more than you need.", "breakfast_a": "Whichever breakfast felt best this week — worth repeating.", "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": "Whichever lunch from this week you''d want again.", "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": "Whichever snack worked best for you this week.", "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": "Whichever dinner from this week you''d want again.", "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": "A familiar snack, whatever suits you."}]'::jsonb,
   'This was the first week about something you drink rather than something you eat — did it change how you noticed thirst, urine colour or the times of day when drinking was easiest to forget?',
   'Next week brings another everyday nutrient your body relies on, found in foods you likely already eat.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 33: Omega-6, the essential fat's other half
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   33,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'Omega-6, the essential fat''s other half',
   '"An essential fat this series saved for last — steady, ordinary, and already part of how you cook."',
   'Linoleic acid (omega-6) is one of only two fats your body cannot make on its own — the other is the omega-3 fat covered back in Week 11. It''s a genuine structural component of every cell membrane, the raw material your body uses to build arachidonic acid and related signalling molecules, and it''s required for normal skin-barrier function. Reference amount: ICMR-NIN''s 2024 Dietary Guidelines describe a minimum n-6 PUFA intake of about 3% of daily energy — approximately 6.6 g in a 2,000-kcal diet. Because this is energy-based, 6.6 g is an example rather than one fixed requirement for every woman. The US National Academies use a different system, giving an Adequate Intake for linoleic acid of 12 g a day for non-pregnant adult women and 13 g a day during pregnancy and lactation. These Adequate Intakes are population reference values based largely on observed intakes, not precise personal targets that need to be counted. Where it naturally shows up: Cooking oils vary widely in linoleic acid: sunflower, safflower, corn, and soybean oil are all rich sources; sesame oil and groundnut (peanut) oil provide a moderate amount; mustard oil, coconut oil, and ghee contain comparatively little. Walnuts and sunflower seeds are good non-oil sources too. None of these are wrong to use — the point isn''t to switch your household''s usual oil, just to know roughly where linoleic acid comes from if you''re curious. About cooking oil: Omega-6 is essential, but oil is still energy-dense. Use enough for cooking and satisfaction without adding extra merely for this nutrient. Avoid repeatedly reheating or reusing frying oil. If oil has smoked heavily, darkened markedly, become foamy, or developed an unpleasant smell, discard it rather than saving it for another meal. The gentle part: Most ordinary Indian diets already include enough linoleic acid through the cooking oil, nuts, and seeds already in regular use — this is one of the more reassuring weeks in this series. Each day below still offers two options for every meal, same as any other week — pick whichever suits you. This week isn''t about switching your cooking oil or adding anything new — most ordinary meals already include this nutrient without any special effort. If you are managing a health condition - Thyroid conditions, diabetes, recent gestational diabetes, high blood pressure, or PCOS: None of these require a special change to the type of fat you eat specifically because of this week''s nutrient. Continue the eating pattern, medication, and monitoring already advised for your condition. If you''ve been told to manage saturated fat or cholesterol for a heart-related reason, that''s a separate conversation with your care team, not something this chart can substitute for.',
   NULL,
   NULL,
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "This week''s nutrient is omega-6, specifically linoleic acid — an essential fat, alongside omega-3 back in Week 11, that your body cannot make on its own.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable adai with curd.", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with whole-wheat toast.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with mixed-vegetable curry, a small bowl of dal and cucumber salad.", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a tomato-onion salad.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small handful of unsalted sunflower seeds with a seasonal fruit.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a seasonal fruit.", "dinner_a": "Millet vegetable appe with mint chutney.", "dinner_b": "A curry made with a locally available fish from the lower-mercury category, with rice and a carrot sabzi.", "still_hungry": "Whatever''s easiest to reach for right now."}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Why breastfeeding is part of this week''s story", "notes": "If you are breastfeeding or expressing milk: The US National Academies'' Adequate Intake for linoleic acid is 13 g a day during lactation, compared with 12 g for a non-pregnant adult woman. These are population references, not numbers you need to count meal by meal. The fatty-acid pattern of breast milk can reflect the mother''s diet to some extent. This does not mean that eating extra omega-6 produces more milk or better milk, and it is not a reason to change oils when the usual diet is already varied.", "breakfast_a": "Dal paratha with a small bowl of curd.", "breakfast_b": "Masala omelette (two eggs, cooked through) with whole-wheat toast.", "lunch_a": "Vegetable oats idli with sambar.", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with roti and a green-peas sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small handful of peanuts or roasted chana with a seasonal fruit.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with almonds.", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a rajma curry and a bottle-gourd sabzi.", "dinner_b": "Chicken stew (cooked through, no stock cubes) with rice and a spinach sabzi.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Where it naturally shows up", "notes": "Linoleic acid is widely available in ordinary cooking oils, nuts and seeds. Different oils contain different amounts, but you do not need to change or rotate oils merely to pursue this nutrient. If you have a food allergy: If you have a diagnosed egg, dairy, peanut, tree-nut, seed, fish, or shellfish allergy, avoid that food completely and choose a tolerated alternative from this week''s chart. If a food causes digestive discomfort rather than an allergy, use only forms and portions you already know you tolerate. Do not trial a problem food to pursue this week''s nutrient.", "breakfast_a": "Palak-corn paratha with curd.", "breakfast_b": "A boiled egg (cooked through) with whole-wheat toast.", "lunch_a": "Kala-chana-and-vegetable patties with phulka, tomato-cucumber salad and a small bowl of curd.", "lunch_b": "A curry made with a locally available fish from the lower-mercury category, with rice and a bhindi sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted chana with fruit, or a modest homemade chana mixture.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of yogurt with a seasonal fruit.", "dinner_a": "Mixed-bean salad bowl with a phulka.", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry (cooked through) with rice and a cabbage sabzi.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "More isn''t automatically the goal", "notes": "Omega-6 is sometimes described online as automatically \"inflammatory,\" but ordinary linoleic-acid intake has not been shown to require avoidance. Omega-6 and omega-3 are both essential. The practical goal is a varied overall diet — not eliminating one family of fats or calculating a ratio at every meal. You do not need several oils in the kitchen or a different oil for each dish. Continue using an oil that suits your household, cooking method, budget, and clinical guidance. Variety can come from the whole diet — including nuts, seeds, legumes, and fish — not from constantly rotating oil bottles.", "breakfast_a": "Paneer-and-peas bhurji with phulka.", "breakfast_b": "Chicken bhurji (cooked through) with whole-wheat toast.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a chana-and-vegetable curry and a carrot salad.", "lunch_b": "Mutton keema (cooked through) with whole-wheat roti and a beans sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "A small bowl of curd with a seasonal fruit.", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted makhana with a small bowl of curd.", "dinner_a": "Vegetable one-pot rice with a cucumber raita.", "dinner_b": "Egg curry (cooked through) with rice and a green-peas sabzi.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "When ordinary meals already cover it", "notes": "True essential-fat deficiency is extremely rare in people eating ordinary food. A wound that is painful, opening, increasingly red, swollen, draining, or not healing as expected needs clinical assessment — not extra oil, nuts, or seeds.", "breakfast_a": "Semolina-and-vegetable uttapam with tomato chutney and a small bowl of curd.", "breakfast_b": "Egg dosa (fully cooked) with vegetable sambar.", "lunch_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a toor dal and a mixed-vegetable soup on the side.", "lunch_b": "A curry made with a locally available fish from the lower-mercury category, with millet pulao and a cabbage sabzi.", "nourishment_break_a": "Fresh fruit with a small unsalted seed-and-nut mixture, using only ingredients already known to be tolerated.", "nourishment_break_b": "Fresh fruit with roasted peanuts.", "dinner_a": "Whole-wheat roti with a moong dal and a carrot sabzi.", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry (cooked through) with rice and a beans sabzi.", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack."}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Away from home", "notes": "You do not need to choose restaurant or packaged food for omega-6. When eating away from home, select whatever balanced meal suits you and focus on freshness, food safety, and overall comfort. Food safety: Roasted chana, peanuts, and whole uncut fruit are shelf-stable. The stuffed parathas, egg wrap, chicken sandwich, and lassi should follow the time-and-temperature guidance above — refrigerated or in an insulated bag with an ice pack until eaten, discarded if left at room temperature for more than about two hours, or about one hour in hot weather. Reheat dinner until steaming hot.", "breakfast_a": "A freshly prepared dal-stuffed paratha with fruit. Carry it in a clean container and eat it within about two hours, or about one hour in hot weather; otherwise keep it cold in an insulated bag.", "breakfast_b": "An egg-and-vegetable wrap (fully cooked egg, whole-wheat wrap), carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten.", "lunch_a": "A paneer-and-vegetable stuffed paratha, carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten.", "lunch_b": "A pre-made chicken sandwich (whole-wheat bread, fully cooked chicken), carried in an insulated bag with an ice pack, kept cold until eaten, with a cucumber side.", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted chana or peanuts, kept portable — no cold storage needed.", "nourishment_break_b": "A small flask of homemade lassi, kept cold.", "dinner_a": "A reheated rajma curry with rice.", "dinner_b": "A reheated mutton curry with rice and a potato sabzi.", "still_hungry": "Whatever''s already within reach."}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "A quiet week to notice what already felt manageable, and what you''d want to carry into the weeks ahead. Still in Settling into strength. Omega-6 turned out to be one of the quieter weeks in this series — not because it matters less, but because most ordinary Indian cooking already covers it without you needing to think about it.", "breakfast_a": "Whichever breakfast felt best this week — worth repeating.", "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": "Whichever lunch from this week you''d want again.", "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": "Whichever snack worked best for you this week.", "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": "Whichever dinner from this week you''d want again.", "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": "A familiar snack, whatever suits you."}]'::jsonb,
   'This week was mostly reassurance: did it help to learn that an ordinary varied diet generally supplies omega-6 without changing oils or adding extra fat?',
   'Next week brings another everyday nutrient your body relies on, found in foods you likely already eat.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
