-- postpartum week 40: Back at work, without skipping yourself
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   40,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'Back at work, without skipping yourself',
   '"Everything this series has covered still matters — the real challenge now is fitting it into a day that isn''t only yours anymore."',
   'This week doesn''t introduce a new nutrient. It''s about protecting everything this series has already covered — protein, iron, water, and the rest — now that paid or unpaid work has taken back a large share of your time. Returning to paid work can happen at very different times — or not at all. By this stage, some mothers have been back for months, some are returning now, and others are doing paid work from home, shift work, family work or unpaid caregiving. Whatever the arrangement, competing demands can make regular meals and water the first things to slip. Know what may apply in your workplace: India''s Maternity Benefit Act provides specific protections in covered establishments. These include two nursing breaks in addition to the normal rest interval until the child reaches 15 months, and a crèche requirement for establishments with 50 or more employees, with up to four visits a day including the rest interval. The maximum maternity-benefit period is generally 26 weeks, but the Act provides 12 weeks for a woman who already has two or more surviving children. Where the work can be performed from home, an employer may allow work from home after maternity benefit for a period and on conditions mutually agreed with the employee. Coverage and eligibility depend on the establishment, employment arrangement and applicable law or policy. Ask HR, the appropriate labour authority or a workers'' support service about your own situation rather than assuming that every provision automatically applies — or that it does not apply.',
   'If you use insulin or glucose-lowering medicine: Do not routinely delay or skip meals without following your diabetes plan. Carry the fast-acting carbohydrate recommended by your care team for low blood glucose, keep it accessible rather than inside a distant lunchbox, and make sure someone at work knows how to help if you develop severe symptoms. If you have a prescribed meal schedule, kidney condition, significant food allergy or another condition affected by meal timing: Build the packed-workday plan around that existing guidance. A convenient office snack should not replace a prescribed treatment or emergency plan.',
   'Cooked egg, chicken, mutton and fish can all be packed for work, but they still need safe temperature control. Keep them cold in a refrigerator or an insulated lunch bag with sufficient frozen ice packs, or keep them continuously hot in a food-grade insulated container designed for hot food. A dry preparation may be easier to pack, but "dry" does not mean shelf-stable.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Whenever and however work resumes — paid or unpaid, in an office or at home — eating regularly for yourself is often the first habit to slip. Not because it stopped mattering, but because there''s genuinely less time, fewer reminders, and more competing demands.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable poha with peanuts and curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg, onion and tomato sandwich, whole wheat, keep properly chilled in a refrigerator or insulated lunch bag with sufficient frozen ice packs until eaten", "lunch_a": "Vegetable curd rice, packed cold in an insulated lunch bag with sufficient frozen ice packs, and refrigerated on arrival if possible", "lunch_b": "Chicken-vegetable millet bowl, packed hot in a preheated insulated container that keeps it hot until lunch", "nourishment_break_a": "A boiled sweet potato with chaat masala and a small handful of roasted chana", "nourishment_break_b": "A small box of roasted chana and peanuts", "dinner_a": "Rice with a moong dal curry and a sautéed cabbage sabzi", "dinner_b": "Fish cutlets with lemon rice and cooked vegetables", "still_hungry": "An apple with a spoon of unsweetened peanut butter"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Meal prep that actually saves time", "notes": "Cooking once and eating it across two or three meals is one of the most realistic ways to keep eating well on a tighter schedule. Dals, curries and khichdi-style dishes generally reheat and freeze well; keeping cut vegetables or a soaked batter ready in the fridge cuts down on decision-making during a rushed morning. Cool batch-cooked food promptly in shallow containers and refrigerate it within two hours — sooner in very hot conditions. Use refrigerated cooked leftovers within three to four days, or freeze portions intended for later. Thaw safely in the refrigerator and reheat until steaming hot. Label containers with the preparation date so \"made earlier\" does not quietly become \"kept too long.\" Egg cups, cooked meat, soaked batter, and cut vegetables all need to stay refrigerated until you''re ready to use them.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable-stuffed paratha, made ahead and reheated, with curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable baked egg cups with whole-wheat toast, kept refrigerated until reheated or eaten", "lunch_a": "Millet-lentil handvo, reheated, with a cooked vegetable side", "lunch_b": "Mutton keema and pea stuffed roti, reheated until steaming", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with a spoon of unsweetened almond butter", "nourishment_break_b": "A small box of trail mix — roasted chana, peanuts, and raisins", "dinner_a": "Batch-cooked chana dal with roti and a cooked vegetable sabzi", "dinner_b": "Batch-cooked chicken curry with rice and a cooked vegetable sabzi", "still_hungry": "A glass of milk with a few almonds"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Keeping a packed lunch safe", "notes": "A lunch packed in the morning and eaten several hours later needs the same food-safety care as any other meal, not less. Worth knowing: Pack perishable meals using one of two safe systems: keep cold food properly chilled in a refrigerator or an insulated lunch bag with enough frozen ice packs, or place piping-hot food in a preheated food-grade insulated container that keeps it hot until lunch. Reheat refrigerated food until steaming hot when possible. Do not assume that dal–rice, roti–sabzi, cooked egg or a dry meat preparation is safe for an entire commute and work morning at room temperature. As a general rule, cooked and perishable food should not remain at room temperature for more than two hours — and only one hour in very hot conditions.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge with milk, topped with a few chopped nuts and a small piece of fruit", "breakfast_b": "A boiled egg with a vegetable sandwich, keep properly chilled in a refrigerator or insulated lunch bag with sufficient frozen ice packs until eaten", "lunch_a": "Dal-rice, packed piping hot in a preheated insulated container that keeps it hot until lunch", "lunch_b": "Fish tikka wrap with cooked vegetables, packed cold in an insulated bag with sufficient ice packs", "nourishment_break_a": "A sliced guava with a small handful of cashews", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of roasted makhana mixed with roasted chana", "dinner_a": "Lemon quinoa with cooked chickpeas and a cucumber salad", "dinner_b": "Rice with a chicken curry and a sautéed spinach", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of papaya with curd"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Something ready for a feeding or pumping break", "notes": "A nursing or pumping break doesn''t leave much time to prepare food — having something ready to simply pick up matters more than it sounds, as long as it''s still been kept cold or hot until then.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma with sambar", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji wrap, keep properly chilled in a refrigerator or insulated lunch bag with sufficient frozen ice packs until eaten", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal curry and a sautéed bhindi (okra)", "lunch_b": "Grilled chicken and vegetable skewers with lemon rice, packed hot in a preheated insulated container", "nourishment_break_a": "A whole seasonal fruit with a small handful of pumpkin seeds", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted chana–makhana mix", "dinner_a": "Multigrain roti with a lauki-chana dal curry", "dinner_b": "Mutton-and-vegetable millet khichdi", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk with a pinch of roasted cumin"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Not losing track of water", "notes": "Busy workdays can make it easy to miss normal thirst cues or postpone drinking. Keeping water visible and linking drinking to routine moments can help. Keeping a water bottle within sight at your desk, and refilling it at a few fixed points in the day — arrival, after lunch, mid-afternoon — tends to work better than trying to remember on your own. Fluid needs vary with weather, activity, breastfeeding, illness and medical conditions. Unless you have been given a fluid restriction, drink regularly and use thirst and urine colour as practical cues rather than forcing a fixed large volume.", "breakfast_a": "Oats-and-vegetable idli with sambar", "breakfast_b": "Masala egg omelette with multigrain toast", "lunch_a": "Rice with a masoor dal curry and a sautéed green beans sabzi", "lunch_b": "Adai with a cooked vegetable chutney and pan-seared fish, packed hot in a preheated insulated container", "nourishment_break_a": "A sliced orange with a small handful of walnuts", "nourishment_break_b": "A glass of coconut water with a small handful of roasted chana", "dinner_a": "Vegetable khichdi with curd", "dinner_b": "Mutton stew with appam and a cooked vegetable side", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk with a few mint leaves"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Away from home — canteen or ordering in", "notes": "Office canteens and food-delivery options don''t always offer an obviously balanced meal, and that''s all right — a reasonable choice most days matters more than a perfect one occasionally. Food safety: When eating out, choose freshly cooked food served hot. Raw salad is reasonable only where washing water, preparation and cold storage are trustworthy; otherwise choose a cooked vegetable or whole fruit you can wash or peel yourself.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable sandwich with hummus and grated paneer filling, keep properly chilled in a refrigerator or insulated lunch bag with sufficient frozen ice packs until eaten", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich, whole wheat, keep properly chilled in a refrigerator or insulated lunch bag with sufficient frozen ice packs until eaten", "lunch_a": "Canteen-style dal–rice with a cooked vegetable side; choose raw salad only where washing and cold storage are reliable", "lunch_b": "Canteen-style chicken curry with rice and a cooked vegetable side", "nourishment_break_a": "A fruit bought fresh, whichever is available, with a small handful of nuts", "nourishment_break_b": "A small packet of roasted chana", "dinner_a": "Reheated vegetable pulao with raita", "dinner_b": "Reheated mutton curry with rice and a cooked vegetable side", "still_hungry": "A glass of milk with a date"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Notice what actually worked this week — which packed meal survived the commute, which snack you reached for without thinking — and which idea sounded good on paper but didn''t fit your real day.", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one thing you did this week — a packed lunch, a snack kept ready, a bottle refilled without thinking — that you''d like to keep doing once this week is over?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at a different kind of everyday habit that quietly shapes how you eat.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 41: Eating well when you're running on little sleep
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   41,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'Eating well when you''re running on little sleep',
   '"Being exhausted doesn''t mean your body needs less food — it often means it needs steadier food, at times you can actually remember to eat it."',
   'This week doesn''t introduce a new nutrient. It''s about eating steadily through a stretch of broken or short sleep, which is still the reality for many mothers at this stage. Research on sleep and hunger hormones like ghrelin and leptin is genuinely mixed — some studies find short sleep changes these hormones in ways that increase hunger, others don''t find a consistent effect. What''s more consistently true, and probably more useful, is behavioural: exhaustion makes it easier to skip meals, forget to eat until you''re overly hungry, or reach for whatever''s fastest — often sugar and caffeine — rather than what actually steadies you. This week is about making the easy choice also a reasonably good one, not about willpower.',
   'If exhaustion feels persistent, overwhelming or out of proportion to the sleep you are getting — or comes with breathlessness, dizziness, palpitations, marked weakness, feeling unusually cold, low mood, intense anxiety or difficulty functioning: mention it to a healthcare professional. Sleep loss may be part of the picture, but anaemia, thyroid problems, depression, anxiety and other conditions can also contribute. Food and rest should not be expected to diagnose or correct these on their own. If you have thoughts of harming yourself or the baby, feel unable to keep either of you safe, feel detached from reality, or see or hear things that others do not, seek urgent emergency help now and involve a trusted adult who can stay with you and the baby. If you use insulin or a glucose-lowering medicine: follow your existing diabetes plan, and ask your treating team what to do if disrupted sleep or unpredictable meals repeatedly affect your glucose readings. Do not change medication doses solely from this programme. If you have a diagnosed food allergy: avoid that food completely and use an alternative you already tolerate. Do not trial or reintroduce it simply because it is convenient during a tired week. If your baby has a diagnosed food allergy: follow the baby''s paediatric or allergy plan. Do not automatically remove the food from your own diet while breastfeeding unless the baby''s treating professional has specifically advised maternal avoidance.',
   'This week''s strategy can work with vegetarian or non-vegetarian foods. Dal, beans, soy, dairy, eggs, fish, chicken and meat can each contribute protein, but they are not nutritionally identical and do not share the same storage needs. Choose foods you tolerate and can store safely. Cooked egg, meat, fish and dairy-based dishes must be refrigerated promptly and kept cold until reheated or eaten.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "There''s no single fix for a short-sleep stretch, and this week isn''t pretending to offer one. The aim is smaller: make it a little easier to eat something steady, even on the days you barely remember eating at all.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia (broken wheat porridge) with milk and a small handful of almonds", "breakfast_b": "Pesarattu roll with an egg filling", "lunch_a": "Rice with a sambar and a sautéed pumpkin sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry with jeera rice and a cucumber salad", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with a small handful of walnuts", "nourishment_break_b": "Dates filled with unsweetened nut butter", "dinner_a": "Khichdi with a spoon of ghee and curd", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry with steamed rice and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk, if you would like one"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Meals that don''t need a rested brain", "notes": "On the nights sleep genuinely doesn''t happen, the best food is whatever needs the least thinking the next day. Preparing something the evening before, or deliberately cooking extra to reheat, removes a decision you may not have the bandwidth for. Refrigerate food prepared the night before promptly. Store cooked leftovers in shallow covered containers within two hours — within one hour in very hot conditions — and use refrigerated leftovers within three to four days. Reheat cooked leftovers until steaming hot. Never leave milk-based oats, cooked eggs, rice, dal, meat or prepared meals on the counter overnight. Worth knowing: Once a can or pouch of fish is opened, eat it promptly or refrigerate the remainder in a clean covered container — do not store leftovers in the opened can. Avoid dented, leaking, rusted, swollen or otherwise damaged cans or pouches.", "breakfast_a": "Overnight oats with milk and grated apple, prepared in a clean covered container and refrigerated overnight", "breakfast_b": "Fully cooked boiled eggs, refrigerated within two hours of cooking and kept refrigerated overnight, with whole-wheat toast", "lunch_a": "Leftover dal-rice with a cooked vegetable, refrigerated safely and reheated until steaming", "lunch_b": "Leftover chicken curry with rice and a cooked vegetable, refrigerated safely and reheated until steaming", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with a small handful of pistachios", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a spoon of roasted sunflower seeds", "dinner_a": "Curd rice with a simple tempering, roasted chana and a cooked vegetable side", "dinner_b": "An unopened shelf-stable pouch or undamaged can of lower-mercury fish, checked for its expiry date and storage instructions, served with rice and a quickly cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A slice of bread with peanut butter"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Using caffeine thoughtfully on a short-sleep day", "notes": "If you use coffee or tea on a short-sleep day, include it within the programme''s total caffeine limit of under 200 mg a day while breastfeeding, unless your maternity team advises otherwise. Check serving size and caffeine content where available; coffee, tea, cola, chocolate and energy drinks can all contribute. Energy drinks can contain large and variable caffeine amounts along with other stimulant ingredients, so they are not a reliable way to manage postpartum exhaustion. Caffeine passes into breast milk in small amounts. Most babies are not noticeably affected by modest maternal intake, but some may appear more wakeful, irritable or unsettled — particularly with larger amounts or caffeine taken late in the day. If you notice a consistent pattern, try reducing the amount or moving it earlier.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi dosa with coconut chutney and sambar", "breakfast_b": "Egg and vegetable semolina muffins", "lunch_a": "Barley-moong vegetable soup with toast", "lunch_b": "Chicken and vegetable barley stew", "nourishment_break_a": "Roasted corn and chickpea chaat", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of fully cooked sprouted moong salad", "dinner_a": "Sattu and vegetable cheela with curd", "dinner_b": "Mutton keema and vegetable dalia", "still_hungry": "A familiar decaffeinated drink with a few dates; check the label because decaffeinated does not always mean completely caffeine-free"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Something by the bed for a night feed", "notes": "A night feed or pumping session isn''t the time to head to the kitchen. Keeping one sealed, shelf-stable snack and a filled water bottle within arm''s reach means you don''t have to choose between resting and eating. During a night feed, sit upright and make sure you are fully awake before eating, especially if the snack contains whole nuts or another food that could be a choking hazard. Reseal or remove leftover food afterwards so crumbs and open packages are not left beside the bed. Worth knowing: Only keep sealed, shelf-stable food by the bed — nuts, dry crackers, a snack bar — never anything perishable like milk, curd, or a prepared dish left out overnight. Keep a water bottle within reach too.", "breakfast_a": "Moong dal and vegetable cheela with curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg sandwich, eaten fresh at breakfast rather than stored overnight", "lunch_a": "Black-eyed-pea and millet bowl", "lunch_b": "Fish and sweet-potato cakes with cooked vegetables", "nourishment_break_a": "A small box of roasted makhana and roasted chana", "nourishment_break_b": "A small sealed packet of trail mix — nuts, seeds, and dried fruit — kept by the bed", "dinner_a": "Roti with a mixed-vegetable-and-chana curry", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry with rice and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A sealed portion of plain whole-grain crackers or another familiar shelf-stable snack, kept by the bed for a night feed"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Combinations that hold you steadier", "notes": "Pairing a carbohydrate food with protein, fibre or both can make a snack or meal more substantial and may help it feel satisfying for longer. It is a practical combination, not a guarantee that tiredness or cravings will disappear.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable-lentil mug dhokla", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable omelette with multigrain toast", "lunch_a": "Red-rice curd bowl with cooked beetroot and roasted chana", "lunch_b": "Mutton keema with roti and a cucumber-tomato salad", "nourishment_break_a": "Banana-oat yoghurt bowl", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of roasted chana and pumpkin seeds", "dinner_a": "Multigrain roti with a chana-and-vegetable curry", "dinner_b": "Paneer-pea rice bowl", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd with a few walnuts"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When someone else is cooking", "notes": "When someone else is cooking, accepting help can reduce the mental load. You do not need to control every dish, but you can use a simple anchor: include a staple, a protein source and some fruit or vegetables where available. It is also reasonable to decline or modify food because of an allergy, medical condition, appetite, preference or food-safety concern — accepting help does not remove your choices.", "breakfast_a": "A home-style breakfast, with curd, sambar or another available protein accompaniment and a piece of fruit where practical", "breakfast_b": "A fully cooked home-style egg dish with roti, dosa or another staple and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_a": "A home-style plate containing rice or roti, dal or beans, and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "A home-style chicken, fish, egg or meat curry with rice or roti and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A whole seasonal fruit with nuts or seeds already known to be tolerated", "nourishment_break_b": "Buttermilk or curd with roasted chana, kept refrigerated until eaten", "dinner_a": "A simple dal-and-vegetable khichdi with curd", "dinner_b": "A home-cooked chicken, fish, egg or meat dish with a staple and cooked vegetables", "still_hungry": "A familiar snack that suits your appetite and health needs"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. If sleep has been genuinely hard this week, notice that without judging it — and notice, too, any small thing that made eating a little easier.", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one small change this week that made it a little easier to eat, even on a tired day?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at a different kind of everyday habit that quietly shapes how you eat.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
