-- postpartum week 42: When everyone has an opinion about what you should eat
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   42,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'When everyone has an opinion about what you should eat',
   '"Plenty of people will tell you what to eat right now — the most useful filter is still what actually works for you and your body."',
   'This week doesn''t introduce a new nutrient. It''s about sorting through the advice — some useful, some not — that tends to arrive from every direction at this stage. By now, you''ve probably heard a lot of confident opinions about what you should or shouldn''t be eating — often from people who care about you. Some of it is genuinely useful. Some of it is folklore repeated so often it sounds like fact. For established lactation, regular and effective milk removal through feeding or expressing helps maintain production. But actual or perceived low supply can have several causes, including feeding frequency, how effectively the baby transfers milk, pump fit or technique, medications, hormonal or medical conditions, and the baby naturally taking fewer milk feeds as complementary foods increase. A supply difficulty is not proof that a mother has eaten incorrectly or failed to try hard enough. If you are mixed feeding, exclusively formula feeding or have already weaned, you can skip the milk-supply sections and use the parts of this week that help with food pressure and unsolicited advice. How your baby is fed is not a measure of your effort or worth, and you do not owe anyone an explanation at the table.',
   'If you are worried that your baby is not getting enough milk: at this stage, the most useful assessment considers the baby''s growth trajectory, hydration, feeding pattern, complementary-food intake where age-appropriate, and how effectively milk is transferred during feeding. Nappy patterns can contribute information, but stool frequency and how full your breasts feel are not reliable stand-alone tests at approximately ten months postpartum. A paediatrician or appropriately qualified lactation professional can assess the complete picture. Seek prompt medical advice if the baby is unusually sleepy or difficult to wake, feeds markedly less than usual, has clearly fewer wet nappies, has a dry mouth, produces no tears when crying, repeatedly vomits, or otherwise appears unwell. If you have diabetes or use insulin or glucose-lowering medicine: celebration foods can sometimes fit within your individual plan, but do not change medication, deliberately skip meals or use fenugreek supplements to correct a glucose reading. Fenugreek in concentrated amounts may lower blood glucose and interact with treatment. Follow your existing plan and seek individual advice if gatherings repeatedly make glucose management difficult. If you have a diagnosed food allergy: avoid that food completely and choose an option you already tolerate. If your baby has a diagnosed food allergy: follow the baby''s paediatric or allergy plan. Do not automatically remove that food from your own diet while breastfeeding unless the baby''s treating professional has specifically advised maternal avoidance.',
   'Meat, fish and eggs can contribute protein and other nutrients, just as well-planned vegetarian foods can. What is unsupported is the claim that a mother must add — or completely avoid — these foods specifically to guarantee milk production. Continue the dietary pattern that suits your preferences, health needs and tolerances.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "You''ll likely keep hearing food advice for as long as you''re breastfeeding, and probably a while after. This week isn''t about proving anyone wrong — it''s about having a calmer way to sort through what''s said to you.", "breakfast_a": "Poha with fully cooked moong sprouts and vegetables, served with curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg curry with vegetable idiyappam", "lunch_a": "Barley and black-eyed-pea bowl", "lunch_b": "Chicken and lentil stew", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with unsweetened seed butter", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of roasted soy and makhana", "dinner_a": "Paneer and corn jowar pockets", "dinner_b": "Fish curry with steamed rice and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd with a few walnuts"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Fenugreek, shatavari and \"milk-booster\" products", "notes": "Fenugreek and shatavari are commonly promoted for milk supply, but the evidence does not support promising a reliable benefit — particularly for established lactation at Postpartum Week 42. Some studies concern the first days after birth, use small samples or test a particular extract that cannot automatically be equated with every powder, tea or capsule sold under the same herb name. Fenugreek leaves or small culinary quantities of seed can remain part of ordinary food if you already eat and tolerate them. Concentrated fenugreek powders, teas, extracts and capsules are different: they can cause gastrointestinal symptoms and allergic reactions, may cross-react in people allergic to peanuts, chickpeas or related legumes, may lower blood glucose, and can interact with medicines including warfarin. Shatavari products also vary in preparation, dose and quality. Evidence from one specific extract or early-postpartum study does not establish that every shatavari product is effective or appropriate at ten months postpartum. Do not begin a concentrated galactagogue product solely because someone says every breastfeeding mother needs it. If supply is genuinely concerning, assess feeding and possible causes rather than using an herb as a substitute for evaluation. If you have diabetes, a legume allergy, take warfarin or another medicine, or are considering a multi-herb product, check it with a qualified healthcare professional or pharmacist before use. Methi (fenugreek leaf) thepla appears on today''s menu as an ordinary dish, not as a milk-supply treatment.", "breakfast_a": "Methi (fenugreek leaf) thepla with curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable paniyaram", "lunch_a": "Pumpkin-chana soup with millet toast", "lunch_b": "Mutton keema-moong handvo", "nourishment_break_a": "Oats porridge with milk and a few chopped almonds", "nourishment_break_b": "A cooked corn-and-black-chana chaat", "dinner_a": "Vegetable-and-paneer stuffed paratha with curd", "dinner_b": "Chicken stew with appam", "still_hungry": "A familiar warm drink or warm milk, if you would like one"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "\"This will spoil your milk\" — the restriction myths", "notes": "You may hear that cold food, certain vegetables, spicy food, or particular combinations will \"spoil\" your milk or upset your baby. Most of these specific claims don''t hold up well under evidence, and broadly restricting your diet on their basis isn''t usually necessary. If you notice symptoms that seem connected with something you ate, record what happened and discuss it with the baby''s healthcare professional rather than beginning a broad elimination diet or deliberately repeating the food as a test. Symptoms such as breathing difficulty, swelling, widespread hives, repeated vomiting, marked lethargy or collapse require urgent medical assessment. Blood in the stool, persistent vomiting, poor growth or ongoing feeding difficulty also deserves professional evaluation.", "breakfast_a": "Rava idli with coconut chutney and sambar", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable paratha", "lunch_a": "Sesame-vegetable rice with cooked soybeans", "lunch_b": "Mutton and vegetable red-rice bowl", "nourishment_break_a": "A bowl of chilled fruit salad (whatever''s in season)", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of curd with a spoon of roasted flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Amaranth and lentil patties with curd", "dinner_b": "Chicken and vegetable millet bowl", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of papaya with a few walnuts"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "A few honest lines for when the advice keeps coming", "notes": "You don''t owe anyone a debate about nutrition science in the middle of a meal. A short, warm response is usually enough: \"Thank you, I''ll keep that in mind,\" \"My doctor''s happy with how things are going,\" \"Thank you for caring — I''m following what works safely for us,\" or simply changing the subject. You can accept the concern behind the comment without adopting the advice itself.", "breakfast_a": "Besan-and-vegetable chilla with curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable frankie", "lunch_a": "Jowar roti with mixed-bean curry and a cooked seasonal vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry with roti and a sautéed vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with a small handful of pistachios", "nourishment_break_b": "A small packet of roasted makhana and soy nuts", "dinner_a": "Multigrain roti with a lauki-chana curry", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry with steamed rice and a stir-fried cabbage", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd with a few dates"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Festivals and family gatherings", "notes": "You do not need to taste every dish to honour the person who prepared it. Choose the foods you genuinely want and that fit your allergy or medical needs. A sweet does not become medically neutral because it is eaten with fruit, and it does not become morally wrong because it is sweet.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable uttapam with sambar", "breakfast_b": "Fully cooked egg-and-vegetable dosa", "lunch_a": "A festival plate with a rice or roti portion, dal or beans, one or two cooked vegetables and curd where tolerated", "lunch_b": "A home-cooked chicken, fish, egg or meat dish with rice or roti and cooked vegetables", "nourishment_break_a": "A small portion of a sweet you genuinely enjoy, if it fits your health needs", "nourishment_break_b": "A seasonal fruit with nuts or seeds already known to be tolerated", "dinner_a": "A lighter home-style dal, vegetable and roti meal", "dinner_b": "Chicken or fish with a staple and cooked vegetables", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When it''s worth actually listening", "notes": "If someone notices that you are repeatedly eating very little, unintentionally losing weight, struggling to function or seeming persistently low or anxious, arrange a health assessment. These changes can be associated with depression, anxiety, thyroid problems, anaemia, digestive illness, food insecurity or other concerns. The observation is a reason to check in — not proof that the observer''s preferred food advice is correct. If you have thoughts of harming yourself or the baby, feel unable to keep either of you safe, feel detached from reality, or see or hear things that others do not, seek urgent emergency help and involve a trusted adult immediately.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable-and-moong-dal dalia with curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-spinach paratha", "lunch_a": "Cooked beetroot-chana chaat with millet roti and curd", "lunch_b": "Grilled chicken with vegetable millet pulao", "nourishment_break_a": "An apple with a small handful of cashews", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of sprouted moong salad, fully cooked", "dinner_a": "Roti with a paneer-and-vegetable curry", "dinner_b": "Mutton stew with rice and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd with a spoon of roasted sunflower seeds"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Think back on the advice you''ve been given this week — what felt kind and worth keeping, and what you''re comfortable letting go of.", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one piece of food advice you''ve received recently that you''re comfortable setting aside, and one you''re glad you kept?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at a different kind of everyday habit that quietly shapes how you eat.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 43: Eating together, now that your baby eats too
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   43,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'Eating together, now that your baby eats too',
   '"The best thing you can model at the table isn''t a perfect plate — it''s a calm one."',
   'This week doesn''t introduce a new nutrient. It''s about the practical and emotional shift that happens once feeding your baby becomes part of your own mealtimes — and making sure that shift doesn''t quietly push your own eating to the side. By Postpartum Week 43, many families have already spent several months navigating complementary feeding, while others may be following a different timeline because of prematurity, development, feeding difficulty or professional advice. This week does not set the baby''s texture, portion or feeding schedule. Its purpose is to stop the work of feeding the baby from repeatedly displacing the mother''s own meal. Feeding two people at the same table, on two different timelines, is its own kind of work — it''s easy for your own meal to become an afterthought: eaten cold, standing up, or skipped while you focus on getting food into your baby. This remains a nourishment plan for the mother. The menus below describe the mother''s complete meal in its normal adult form. If part of a family dish may also be suitable for the baby, portioning, texture, allergen introduction, salt, sugar, honey and choking safety must follow the baby''s own paediatric or feeding guidance. Do not use the adult menu wording as instructions for the baby.',
   'If you are unsure whether your baby is gagging or choking: gagging is a protective reflex and is often noisy. Choking means the airway is partly or completely blocked and may become quiet if the baby cannot breathe or vocalise — it can be difficult to tell the two apart without training. If the baby cannot breathe, cry or cough effectively, changes colour, becomes limp or unresponsive, treat it as an emergency and begin the infant choking response you have been trained to use while emergency help is contacted. If you have not learned infant choking response, consider taking a certified course. Do not perform a blind finger sweep or try to wash food down with water. Persistent gagging, coughing during meals, recurrent choking, a wet or gurgly voice after swallowing, prolonged mealtimes or difficulty progressing textures deserves assessment by the baby''s paediatric or feeding professional. If you have diabetes or use insulin or glucose-lowering medicine: family meals you share with your baby can usually fit within your existing plan — focus on your own portions and timing rather than mirroring your baby''s small, frequent meals. Ask your treating team if irregular mealtimes around your baby''s feeding schedule affect your glucose readings. If you have a diagnosed food allergy: avoid that food completely when preparing or sharing meals, and keep a safe alternative on hand for yourself. If your baby has a diagnosed food allergy or has been advised to avoid a food: follow the baby''s paediatric or allergy plan when deciding what to share from your own plate. Do not offer a restricted food to the baby simply because it is easy to share. When preparing both meals, prevent cross-contact according to the allergy plan — shared serving spoons, chopping boards, pans, spreads and food already touched by an allergen can matter even when the restricted ingredient is not visibly present.',
   'Chicken, fish and eggs can form part of the mother''s meals. Whether the baby can share a particular preparation depends on previous allergen introduction, bones, cooking, texture, salt and the baby''s individual feeding plan. Do not assume that an adult curry, fried fish, meat preparation or egg dish is automatically suitable for the baby. Keep fish to two or three servings a week for yourself, choosing varied, lower-mercury types and cooking it fully.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Mother''s meal remains a real meal", "notes": "Eating alongside your baby can easily turn into bites taken standing up, or whatever''s left once your baby''s plate is done. This week starts with a simple standard: your own meal should be a complete, sit-down plate — not a smaller, interrupted version of one.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable-lentil adai with curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg curry with vegetable appam", "lunch_a": "Savoury millet, paneer and vegetable bowl", "lunch_b": "Chicken-chana pulao with cooked vegetables", "nourishment_break_a": "Fruit with unsweetened yoghurt and seeds", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted soy-makhana mix (adult portion — keep whole roasted soy nuts out of the baby''s reach)", "dinner_a": "Black-chana and vegetable stuffed paratha with curd", "dinner_b": "Fish and vegetable cutlets with red rice", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd with a few walnuts (adult portion — keep out of the baby''s reach)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "Responsive feeding without controlling either plate", "notes": "Responsive feeding means offering safe, age-appropriate food on a reasonably predictable rhythm, noticing the baby''s hunger and fullness cues, encouraging without forcing, and allowing appetite to vary. The caregiver remains responsible for what is safely offered and for seeking help when intake, growth or feeding skills are concerning. The baby''s cues help guide how much is eaten at a particular meal. You also do not need to clean your plate or perform perfect eating for the baby. Your own hunger, fullness and preferences still matter. One lightly eaten or refused meal is not automatically a problem. Repeated refusal, coughing, distress, difficulty managing textures, very restricted intake, poor growth or concern about nutritional adequacy should be discussed with the baby''s healthcare or feeding team.", "breakfast_a": "Ragi porridge with milk and a spoon of mixed seeds", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable uttapam", "lunch_a": "Rajma-vegetable jowar bowl", "lunch_b": "Mutton-vegetable dalia", "nourishment_break_a": "Cooked corn-chana chaat", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd with a spoon of roasted flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Moong dal khichdi with curd and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Chicken curry with rice and a stir-fried cabbage", "still_hungry": "A banana with a small handful of almonds (adult portion — keep out of the baby''s reach)"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "One family dish, separate safe portions", "notes": "If you''re planning to serve one family dish and portion part of it for your baby, set aside the baby''s portion before adding honey or salt and before making any other changes that are not part of the baby''s established feeding plan. Honey must never be given before 12 months. The list below is a quick safety reference for that handoff — not a substitute for your baby''s own feeding guidance. If you plan to share part of a family dish with the baby: - set aside the baby''s portion before adding honey and before adding salt - never give honey before 12 months - use a separate clean utensil and serving portion rather than food already bitten or eaten from - remove bones, stones, pits and other hard pieces - prepare the shape, size and texture for the baby''s current developmental ability and feeding plan - keep whole nuts, whole beans, raisins, popcorn, hard raw fruit or vegetables, round grapes or tomatoes, sausages cut into rounds, and chunks or spoonfuls of nut or seed butter out of the baby''s reach - keep the baby seated upright and closely supervised whenever eating", "breakfast_a": "Idli with sambar and coconut chutney", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable dosa with coconut chutney", "lunch_a": "Mixed-bean and barley stew with toast", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry with rice and a sautéed bhindi (okra)", "nourishment_break_a": "A pear with curd", "nourishment_break_b": "Paneer and cucumber bowl with lemon", "dinner_a": "Roti with a mixed-vegetable curry, dal and curd", "dinner_b": "Fish curry with rice and a cooked spinach sabzi", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd with chopped papaya"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "Feeding alongside, not always feeding baby first", "notes": "It''s common to feed your baby''s entire meal before taking a single bite of your own — by the time you sit down, yours may be cold, or skipped altogether. A few small shifts can help protect your own meal: - plate your own full meal before you start feeding your baby - keep your portion covered so it stays warm rather than accepting it cold as normal - ask another adult to take over for a few minutes so you can actually sit and eat - lean on something that reheats well instead of a meal that goes cold every day - use a separate serving utensil for your own plate where hygiene or allergen concerns apply Eating a proper meal a little later still counts far more than a few interrupted bites eaten standing up. You and your baby don''t have to eat the same food, or at exactly the same time.", "breakfast_a": "Poha with peanuts and curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with multigrain toast", "lunch_a": "Rice noodles with tofu and cooked vegetables", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry with roti and a cucumber-tomato salad", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with a spoon of peanut butter (adult portion — keep spoonfuls of nut butter out of the baby''s reach)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd with grated apple and a spoon of roasted flaxseed", "dinner_a": "Vegetable pulao with raita", "dinner_b": "Mutton and vegetable curry with rice", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "Calm modelling without performance", "notes": "Around this age, many babies watch and sometimes imitate what happens at the table, including your own reactions to food. This tends to shape food acceptance gradually, over many repeated meals — not from any single sitting or a single reaction. There''s no need to perform enthusiasm you don''t feel, or hide a food you genuinely dislike — simply eating your own meal calmly nearby is enough of a model.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable upma with sambar", "breakfast_b": "Moong-corn handvo with mint curd", "lunch_a": "Rice with a chana dal and a bottle-gourd sabzi", "lunch_b": "Chicken and vegetable curry with rice", "nourishment_break_a": "A chopped apple with curd", "nourishment_break_b": "Roasted corn and paneer chaat", "dinner_a": "Jowar roti with a moong dal curry and a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Grilled fish with millet-and-vegetable pulao", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of roasted chana (adult portion — keep out of the baby''s reach)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "When shared meals are not actually easier", "notes": "Sharing one dish across the table doesn''t always work — a spicier family meal, an allergy to plan around, or simply different schedules can make a separate plate the better choice on a given day. That''s a reasonable call, not a failure to make things simpler. A baby-safe meal prepared separately, an allergy-safe version, or eating at a slightly different time from your baby are all legitimate options.", "breakfast_a": "Besan chilla with vegetables and curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable appam with curd", "lunch_a": "Multigrain roti with a mixed-vegetable curry, dal and curd", "lunch_b": "Chicken curry with rice and a stir-fried bell pepper", "nourishment_break_a": "Steamed sweet potato with curd and roasted cumin", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd with banana and a pinch of cinnamon", "dinner_a": "Khichdi (moong dal) with a sautéed vegetable and ghee", "dinner_b": "Mutton keema with rice and a cooked vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Think back on one meal this week that felt easy to share, and one that didn''t — what made the difference?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
   'What''s one thing that would make sharing meals with your baby feel a little easier day to day?',
   'Next week, we''ll look at another everyday pattern worth a closer look.')
on conflict (stage, week_number) do update set
  trimester = excluded.trimester,
  phase_label = excluded.phase_label,
  theme_title = excluded.theme_title,
  mantra = excluded.mantra,
  why_it_matters = excluded.why_it_matters,
  condition_notes = excluded.condition_notes,
  meat_fish_eggs_note = excluded.meat_fish_eggs_note,
  using_meals_note = excluded.using_meals_note,
  days = excluded.days,
  reflection = excluded.reflection,
  looking_ahead = excluded.looking_ahead;

-- postpartum week 44: When you can't think of what to cook anymore
insert into nourish_week_content
  (stage, week_number, trimester, phase_label, theme_title, mantra,
   why_it_matters, condition_notes, meat_fish_eggs_note, using_meals_note,
   days, reflection, looking_ahead)
values
  ('postpartum',
   44,
   NULL,
   'Phase 4 of 6 — Settling into strength',
   'When you can''t think of what to cook anymore',
   '"A repeated meal isn''t a lack of creativity — it''s one less decision on a day that already asked a lot of you."',
   'This week doesn''t introduce a new nutrient. It''s about the daily decision of what to eat — and making that decision smaller, not more elaborate. By nearly a year in, deciding what to eat — every single day, on top of everything else — can wear a person down more than the actual cooking does. This week isn''t about becoming a better cook or planning more elaborately. It''s about giving yourself permission to make that decision smaller: a short, repeatable rotation of meals you already know how to make, rather than a new idea every day.',
   'If you have diabetes or use insulin or glucose-lowering medicine: a repeatable meal rotation may make meal timing and carbohydrate amounts more predictable. The repeated meals still need to fit your established portions, medication timing and individual glucose-management plan. If you have a diagnosed food allergy: a smaller, repeated rotation of meals can make it easier to stay consistently safe, once you have confirmed that each dish and all of its ingredients are safe for you. Recheck packaged ingredient labels occasionally, since formulations can change even for a food you buy regularly. If decision fatigue is turning into skipping meals rather than repeating them, or "I don''t know what to eat" is becoming "I''m not eating," that''s worth naming to someone — a partner, a friend, or your care team — rather than pushing through alone.',
   'A repeatable rotation works just as well for vegetarian and non-vegetarian meals — pick two or three dependable options within your usual protein sources and let them repeat without needing a new idea each time. Keep fish to two or three servings a week, choosing varied, lower-mercury types and cooking it fully.',
   NULL,
   '[{"day_number": 1, "title": "Anchor", "notes": "Today, choose the two or three meals in this week''s plan that already feel familiar. Those — not the most impressive options — are the beginning of your dependable rotation.", "breakfast_a": "Oats idli with coconut chutney and curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable sandwich", "lunch_a": "Rice with a toor dal and a sautéed cabbage sabzi", "lunch_b": "Grilled chicken with a cucumber-tomato salad and rice", "nourishment_break_a": "Chopped watermelon with curd or paneer", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd with a spoon of roasted pumpkin seeds", "dinner_a": "Vegetable biryani with raita", "dinner_b": "Pan-cooked fish with rice and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd with a few cashews (adult portion — keep whole nuts out of the baby''s reach)"}, {"day_number": 2, "title": "A short list of ''good enough'' meals", "notes": "You don''t need a new idea every day. A short list of five or six meals you already know how to make, genuinely enjoy, and can put together without much thought is worth more than a much longer list you never actually use. Repeating a meal isn''t a sign you''ve run out of ideas — it''s one less decision on a day that already asked a lot of you.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha with curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg bhurji with roti", "lunch_a": "Multigrain roti with a chana masala and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry with rice and a stir-fried bell pepper", "nourishment_break_a": "A banana with a spoon of almond butter (adult portion — do not offer spoonfuls of nut butter to the baby)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd with grated cucumber and roasted cumin", "dinner_a": "A stuffed paratha using the sautéed cabbage sabzi saved from Day 1, with curd", "dinner_b": "Chicken tikka with millet-and-vegetable pulao", "still_hungry": "A glass of warm milk"}, {"day_number": 3, "title": "Batch-cooking one thing, twice", "notes": "Cooking a double portion of one dal, curry or grain and reworking it into a second, slightly different meal a day or two later can cut your actual cooking decisions in half without anyone noticing it''s the same base. Some of today''s lunch and dinner options build on what you already cooked earlier this week. Worth knowing: Cool and refrigerate the portion you plan to reuse promptly — within two hours, or within one hour in very hot conditions. Use refrigerated leftovers within three to four days and reheat them until steaming hot.", "breakfast_a": "Jowar porridge with milk and a spoon of chopped nuts (adult portion — chopped nuts aren''t automatically suitable for the baby)", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable dosa", "lunch_a": "Rice with a lauki-chana dal and a sautéed vegetable", "lunch_b": "A wrap made with the grilled chicken saved from Day 1 and a sautéed vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "A chopped pear with curd", "nourishment_break_b": "A small bowl of roasted chana and peanuts (adult portion — keep whole nuts out of the baby''s reach)", "dinner_a": "A dal soup made from the toor dal saved from Day 1, loosened with water and lemon, with toast", "dinner_b": "Mutton keema wrap with a cucumber-tomato salad", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd with chopped papaya"}, {"day_number": 4, "title": "The grocery list that thinks for you", "notes": "A repeating list of staple ingredients — the ones your go-to meals actually use — means grocery shopping stops being its own decision-making exercise too. Keep it written down somewhere you''ll actually see it again, and let it do the thinking the next time you''re too tired to plan from scratch.", "breakfast_a": "Oats idli with coconut chutney and curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable stuffed paratha with curd", "lunch_a": "Jowar roti with a mixed-vegetable curry and dal", "lunch_b": "Pan-seared fish with lemon rice and a cooked vegetable", "nourishment_break_a": "Chopped mango with curd", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd with a spoon of roasted sesame seeds", "dinner_a": "Moong-dal and vegetable khichdi with ghee", "dinner_b": "Chicken stew with rice and sautéed green beans", "still_hungry": "A banana with a small handful of walnuts (adult portion — keep whole nuts out of the baby''s reach)"}, {"day_number": 5, "title": "When ''good enough'' still feels like giving up", "notes": "It''s common to feel a flicker of guilt over a repeated, simple meal — as though it says something about how much effort you''re putting in. A meal that''s nutritionally reasonable and actually gets eaten is doing its job, whether it took an hour or ten minutes to make.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable dalia with curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable dosa", "lunch_a": "Rice with a rajma curry and a cooked vegetable", "lunch_b": "Mutton curry with roti and sautéed spinach", "nourishment_break_a": "Chopped papaya with curd and seeds", "nourishment_break_b": "Paneer and cucumber cubes with chaat masala", "dinner_a": "Roti with a mixed-vegetable curry and curd", "dinner_b": "Baked fish with rice and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A small bowl of curd with a few pistachios (adult portion — keep whole nuts out of the baby''s reach)"}, {"day_number": 6, "title": "Letting someone else decide sometimes", "notes": "You don''t have to be the one who decides every meal, every day. Asking a partner or family member to choose and make dinner occasionally, or ordering something in without a debate about whether you ''should,'' are both reasonable strategies — not signs you''ve stopped trying.", "breakfast_a": "Vegetable paratha with curd", "breakfast_b": "Egg-and-vegetable paratha with curd", "lunch_a": "A stuffed paratha using chana masala saved and frozen promptly from Day 2, thawed safely and reheated until steaming hot, with curd", "lunch_b": "Chicken biryani with raita", "nourishment_break_a": "A chopped apple with a spoon of peanut butter (adult portion — do not offer spoonfuls of nut butter to the baby)", "nourishment_break_b": "Curd with pomegranate and a pinch of chaat masala", "dinner_a": "Khichdi (moong dal) with a sautéed vegetable", "dinner_b": "Mutton curry with rice and a sautéed vegetable", "still_hungry": "A glass of buttermilk"}, {"day_number": 7, "title": "Reflection", "notes": "No forced choices today. Which meal from this week would you happily put on repeat, without needing a reason?", "breakfast_a": null, "breakfast_b": null, "lunch_a": null, "lunch_b": null, "nourishment_break_a": null, "nourishment_break_b": null, "dinner_a": null, "dinner_b": null, "still_hungry": null}]'::jsonb,
